// For God so loved the world that he gave his only begotten Son, that whoever believes in him should not perish but have eternal life.
// Linguistic observations from the LLM merge pass — one JSON object per lemma
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5375","glossChirho":"נשׂא lift/carry","sensesChirho":12,"notesChirho":"Stem (binyan) is primary separator. Within Qal, idiomatic collocations create distinct senses: nasa panim (show partiality), nasa avon (forgive sin vs bear guilt — context-dependent), nasa rosh (take census). The forgive vs endure-consequence split is theologically significant."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7760a","glossChirho":"שׂים put/set","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic جَعَلَ vs وَضَعَ cleanly separates 'make into' (transformative) from 'put/place' (physical). The idiom שׂים לב (set heart = pay attention) is a distinct lexicalized expression."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5674a","glossChirho":"עבר pass/cross","sensesChirho":10,"notesChirho":"Spanish cruzar vs pasar, Korean 건너 vs 지나가, Swahili kuvuka vs kupita all independently distinguish 'cross a boundary' from 'pass through'. Hifil proclaim sense (cause decree to pass through) is a specialized idiom."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1980","glossChirho":"הלך go/walk","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic salaka (follow a path) signals the metaphorical conduct sense vs dhahaba (physical going). Hitpael 'walk about' covers intimate companionship walking (Enoch walked with God, Gen 5:22)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8104","glossChirho":"שׁמר keep/guard","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Qal covers both physical guarding and obedient keeping of commandments in one sense — the multilingual glosses don't consistently separate these. Niphal 'beware/take heed' is reflexive self-guarding."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5493","glossChirho":"סור turn aside","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Hifil splits into physical removal vs moral purging (putting away idols/evil). Hindi utaarī and Korean beot-da (take off garments) mark the garment-removal sub-sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7725","glossChirho":"שׁוב return","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Arabic تُوبُوا (tawba = repentance) and Swahili tubuni distinguished the Qal repentance sense from physical return. Hifil 'return a word' = answer/reply is an important idiom (Spanish respondió, Swahili kujibu)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5975","glossChirho":"עמד stand","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Arabic roots ث-ب-ت/ب-ق-ي/ص-م-د (endure) vs و-ق-ف (stop) separate the endure and stop sub-senses within Qal. Hindi ठहर/रुक (stop) vs बना रह/टिक (endure) confirmed."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4672","glossChirho":"מצא find","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic أَصَابَ (befall/strike) vs وَجَدَ (find) distinguishes 'calamity finds someone' from intentional finding. يَكْفِي (suffice) catches the rare 'be enough' sense. Niphal articulated participle הַנִּמְצְאִים is a lexicalized quasi-nominal 'those present'."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3427","glossChirho":"ישׁב sit/dwell","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Spanish sentarse vs habitar vs quedarse, Arabic جلس vs سكن vs مكث, Korean 앉 vs 거주 vs 머물 all independently three-way-split sit/dwell/remain. Mega-cluster 104422 (539 occ) needed manual override from sit to dwell."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3045","glossChirho":"ידע know","sensesChirho":13,"notesChirho":"Spanish saber vs conocer and Arabic عَلِمَ vs عَرَفَ distinguish cognitive from relational knowledge. Sexual knowing (euphemism) confirmed across all languages. Pual participle 'acquaintance' is a lexicalized nominal."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3381","glossChirho":"ירד go down","sensesChirho":9,"notesChirho":"Hifil splits into concrete bringing-down vs figurative sending-down (rain/judgment) — Arabic anuzilu vs ukhdi'u, Hindi utaarna vs barsaana. 'Those going down to the pit' (yorde bor) is a formulaic prophetic expression."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5046","glossChirho":"נגד tell","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Arabic أَخْبَرَ (inform) vs يُعْلِنُ (announce) separates tell/report from declare/proclaim. Swahili tangaza (announce publicly) vs ambia (tell) confirmed. Rare senses: denounce, confess, disclose each have distinctive multilingual evidence."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5221","glossChirho":"נכה strike","sensesChirho":11,"notesChirho":"Arabic qatala (kill) vs daraba (hit) and Swahili kuua vs kupiga separate lethal from non-lethal striking. Specialized sub-senses: clap hands, thrust/stab, drive in nail, defeat — each confirmed by distinct target-language verbs."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6965b","glossChirho":"קום rise","sensesChirho":12,"notesChirho":"Arabic yathbutu (be firm) vs yaqumu (rise) separates stand firm/endure from basic arising. Hifil splits 5 ways: raise person, erect object, establish covenant, fulfill promise, rouse from inactivity. Arabic jamuda (congeal) catches the rare physical 'stand still' sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8130","glossChirho":"שׂנא hate","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Sonnet collapsed to 2 senses; Opus found 6. Key: Qal participle 'hater/enemy' is a lexicalized nominal (Arabic عدو, Swahili adui) distinct from finite 'to hate'. Qal passive participle שְׂנוּאָה 'unloved wife' is adjectival/stative vs Nifal processual passive."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G0142","glossChirho":"αἴρω lift/take","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Sonnet found 3; Opus found 8. Caught nautical 'weigh anchor' (Acts 27), hostile 'Away with him!' (John 19:15), and idiomatic 'keep in suspense' (John 10:24 τὴν ψυχὴν αἴρω). Korean 들어-올리다 (lift up) vs 가져가다 (take away) was diagnostic."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7069","glossChirho":"קנה acquire/buy","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Spanish comprar vs adquirir vs creador separates buy/purchase from acquire/obtain from create/possess. Gen 14:19 Creator/Possessor of heaven: Arabic خالق, Swahili Muumba. Caught qana'/qanah conflation in Deut 32:21 (provoke to jealousy)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2076","glossChirho":"זבח sacrifice","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Swahili kuchinja (slaughter animal) vs kutoa dhabihu (offer sacrifice) was the cleanest discriminator for the slaughter-vs-sacrifice split. Matches HALOT Qal §1 vs §2-5. Could be refined further with syntactic frames (deity recipients, offering objects)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2428","glossChirho":"חיל strength/army","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Arabic provides 5 distinct lexemes mapping to 5 senses: جيش (army), بأس (valor), ثروة (wealth), قوة (strength), فضيلة (virtue). Korean and Swahili independently confirmed all 5 splits. Best example of multilingual evidence resolving polysemy."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2891","glossChirho":"טהר be clean","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Critical Piel split: declarative 'pronounce clean' (priest's speech act, Lev 13-14) vs factitive 'make clean/purify' (actual removal of impurity). This is a real lexicographic distinction within the same stem."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0929","glossChirho":"בהמה beast/cattle","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Korean 짐승 (wild/generic beast) vs 가축 (domesticated livestock), Spanish bestia vs ganado, Swahili mnyama vs mifugo. Arabic بَهِيمَة covers both — less discriminating here."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G3860","glossChirho":"παραδίδωμι hand over","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Betray (Judas) vs hand over to custody vs give over/surrender vs entrust vs transmit tradition vs yield fruit. 'Transmit tradition' (1 Cor 11:23) is theologically crucial and distinct from betrayal despite same Greek verb. Hindi पकड़वाना (betray) vs सौंपना (hand over) was diagnostic."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1431","glossChirho":"גדל be great/grow","sensesChirho":9,"notesChirho":"Piel splits: 'raise children' (Arabic رَبَّى, Korean 양육하다, Swahili -lea) vs 'magnify/extol' (Arabic عَظَّمَ, Korean 높이다, Swahili -tukuza) — different semantic domains within same stem."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3824","glossChirho":"לבב heart","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"52 of 54 clusters are one sense — glosses entirely uniform. Swahili katikati ya (middle of) vs moyo (heart) caught the rare spatial 'center/midst' sense. One adjectival 'whole/sound' cluster may be a tagging error."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8451","glossChirho":"תורה Torah/law","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Korean 율법 (law) vs 가르침 (teaching) and Swahili sheria vs mafundisho cleanly separate divine Torah from human instruction. Plural תּוֹרֹת morphologically and semantically distinct as individual regulations."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5650","glossChirho":"עבד servant","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Swahili mtumishi (free servant) vs mtumwa (slave/owned person) was the strongest signal for servant/slave distinction. 'Servant of God' honorific (Moses, David) identified by 1cs possessive + comparative preposition pattern."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8179","glossChirho":"שׁער gate","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Deuteronomic 'your gates' = 'your towns' metonymy identified by 2nd-person possessive suffix concentration on plural form. All other inflections are one sense — physical gate/entrance."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4725","glossChirho":"מקום place","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"51 of 52 clusters are one sense. Swahili nafasi (space/room) vs mahali (place) caught the single spatial-extent sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8210","glossChirho":"שׁפך pour out","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic سَكَبَ (pour liquid) vs سَفَكَ (shed blood) is a lexical split in Arabic mapping to a real Hebrew polysemy. Korean 쌓다 (pile up) caught the siege-ramp sense within the metaphorical group."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2181","glossChirho":"זנה commit harlotry","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Literal sexual harlotry and metaphorical spiritual unfaithfulness (prophetic idiom) were merged in Qal — flagged for refinement. Hifil causative confirmed by Spanish hizo fornicar, Hindi व्यभिचार कराया, Swahili akawafanya wazini."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6031b","glossChirho":"ענה afflict/humble","sensesChirho":10,"notesChirho":"Piel splits: afflict/oppress vs humble/bring low vs violate/ravish (sexual, Deut 22). Hitpael self-affliction = fasting. Caught Psalm 88 superscription debate: Arabic لِلْإِنْشَادِ and Korean 노래하기 위하여 support 'to sing/chant' (possible root ענה III)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G2564","glossChirho":"καλέω call","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Korean 초대하다/초대받다 (invite) vs 부르다 (call/summon) separates hospitality invitation from general summoning. The divine vocation sense (Pauline 'call to salvation') confirmed by Hindi बुलाहट (calling/vocation) and contextual divine-agent framing."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5641","glossChirho":"סתר hide/conceal","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Clean binyan separation: Hifil causative hide (30 occ), Nifal reflexive/passive be hidden (28 occ), Hitpael deliberate self-concealment (3 occ), Piel intensive (1 occ), Pual passive-intensive (1 occ). The Nifal participle נסתרות 'hidden things' (Deut 29:29) functions as a lexicalized nominal."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0341","glossChirho":"אֹיֵב enemy","sensesChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Remarkably uniform — all 69 clusters collapsed to a single sense. This frozen participle (Qal ptcp of root א-י-ב) has fully lexicalized as a noun 'enemy/adversary' with no semantic variation across 276 occurrences. Arabic عدو, Swahili adui, Korean 원수/적 all uniformly 'enemy'."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1320","glossChirho":"בשׂר flesh","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Korean 고기 (meat) vs 육체/살 (body) vs Swahili ngozi (skin) independently triangulate the three senses. Arabic لَحْم (meat) vs جَسَد (body) vs بَشَرَة (skin surface) confirmed. The Levitical skin-inspection sense is context-specific to priestly examination texts."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0376","glossChirho":"אישׁ man","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"The distributive sense 'each one/everyone' (7 occ) is confirmed by Arabic كُلّ واحد and Korean 각각/모든사람. The 'husband' sense (4 occ) triggered by feminine possessive suffixes. Biological sex 'male' vs 'female' (1 occ) is the Gen 1:27 usage."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G1453","glossChirho":"ἐγείρω raise","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Active resurrection vs passive resurrection is the most critical theological split. Arabic أَقَامَ (active causative) vs قامَ (intransitive rise) distinguished these. Korean 살리다 (make alive) vs 살아나다 (come alive) confirmed. The 'wake from sleep' sense (4 occ) is literal rather than resurrection metaphor — Arabic أَيقَظ (awaken)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1818","glossChirho":"דם blood","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"The bloodshed/violence metonymy (often plural דמים) consistently triggers Arabic قَتْل/عُنْف (killing/violence) vs literal دَم (blood). Bloodguilt as juridical liability is confirmed by the formula דמו בראשו 'his blood on his head'. Swahili damu covers both literal and metonymic."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4376","glossChirho":"מכר sell","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Clean binyan split. Participle 'seller' lexicalized as professional role (Ezek 7, Neh 13). Hitpael 'sell oneself' has both literal (debt servitude, Lev 25:47) and figurative (selling oneself to do evil, 1 Kgs 21:20-25) sub-senses within one stem."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G2596","glossChirho":"κατά according to/down","sensesChirho":11,"notesChirho":"Most polysemous Greek preposition in our data. The genitive/accusative case distinction creates the primary split: genitive 'against/down from' vs accusative 'according to/throughout/each'. Korean 대로 (according to) vs 을 향해 (toward) vs 대하여 (against) discriminated well. The fixed expressions κατ' ἰδίαν (privately) and καθ' ὑπερβολήν (exceedingly) are true idioms."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7495","glossChirho":"רפא heal","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Participle 'physician/healer' is substantival (Arabic طبيب, Swahili mganga). Piel splits: medical treatment of persons vs physical repair of objects (walls, altars). The Hitpael reflexive 'get oneself healed' (2 Kgs 5:11, King seeking cure) shows subject initiative — distinct from passive Nifal."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5750","glossChirho":"עוד still/again","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"The 'while still' temporal sense (בְּעוֹד with prepositional בְּ) is structurally distinct — Spanish 'mientras todavía' and Korean '아직 ~하는 동안' confirmed. Arabic أَيْضًا (also/again) vs لا يزال (still/continuing) is the primary sense split signal. The 'since one's existence' (מֵעוֹד) temporal origin is rare but distinctive."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0639","glossChirho":"אַף nose/anger","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Classic body-part→emotion metonymy. Arabic أَنْف (nose) vs غَضَب (anger) is the cleanest discriminator. The dual אַפַּיִם serves triple duty: nostrils (literal), face (prostration idiom), and slow-to-anger (אֶרֶךְ אַפַּיִם). Arabic الأَناة 'patience/forbearance' catches the idiom. The 'double/twofold' sense (Isa 40:2) is a hapax use of the dual."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G5100","glossChirho":"τὶς someone/anyone","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Arabic أَحَدٌ (person) vs شَيْئًا (thing) vs بَعْضُ (some/plural) cleanly triangulates the core 3-way split. The adjectival/modifier sense is distinguished by Arabic وَاحِدٌ/أَيُّ and Swahili fulani/mmoja. Single interrogative use (John?) may be an accentual overlap with τίς (G5101)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2534","glossChirho":"חֵמָה wrath/venom","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"True homonymy: 'wrath' (Arabic غَضَب, Swahili ghadhabu/hasira) has zero gloss overlap with 'venom' (Arabic سُمّ, Swahili sumu, Hindi ज़هر). The 'heat' sense (Arabic حَرارَة, Swahili joto) may etymologically underlie both extensions (heat of anger, heat of poison) but is semantically distinct. BDB lists these as separate roots."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0802","glossChirho":"אִשָּׁה woman/wife","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Arabic مَرْأَة/امْرَأَة (woman) vs زَوْجَة (wife) and Korean 여자/여인 (woman) vs 아내 (wife) cleanly separate the two main senses. The distributive 'each woman' (אִשָּׁה...רְעוּתָהּ) confirmed by Arabic واحِدَة. Animal 'female/mate' (Gen 7 ark context) caught by Arabic أُنْثَى and Swahili jike."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G3739","glossChirho":"ὅς who/which","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Most clusters merge into one basic relative pronoun sense — the over-splitting was purely grammatical (case/gender/number). Arabic مَنْ (indefinite who) vs الَّذِي (definite who) catches the whoever/whatever generalizing sense. The correlative ὃς μέν...ὃς δέ pattern (Arabic بَعْضٌ, Korean 어떤 것은) is syntactically not a relative clause at all."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5971a","glossChirho":"עַם people","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"98.9% of occurrences are one sense. Arabic عَمِّي (kinsman) vs شَعْبِي (my people) caught the rare familial/ancestral 'kinfolk' sense in death idioms (נֶאֱסַף אֶל-עַמָּיו). Arabic جَماعَة (troop/band) vs شَعْب (people) caught a single military retinue usage."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4639","glossChirho":"מַעֲשֶׂה work/deed","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic صَنْعَة (craftsmanship) vs عَمَل (labor/deed) and Korean 솜씨 (skill) vs 행위 (conduct) vs 일 (work) triangulate 3 main senses. 'Works of God' in doxological context is a theological register distinct from human deeds. Korean 소출 (produce/yield) caught the product/result sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7489a","glossChirho":"רעע be bad/do evil","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Hifil 'do evil' (41 clusters) is the dominant sense, massively over-fragmented by inflection. Hifil participle מְרֵעִים 'evildoers' fully lexicalized as noun (Arabic الأَشْرار, Korean 행악자). Single Nifal passive and single nominal use each have distinctive gloss profiles."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1197a","glossChirho":"בער burn/purge","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"The Deuteronomic purge formula (בִּעַרְתָּ הָרָע) is cleanly separated from physical burning: Arabic تَنْزِع/أَزَالَ (remove) vs أَشْعَلَ/أَوْقَدَ (kindle). Korean 제거하다 (eliminate) vs 태우다 (burn) confirmed. The Hifil causative kindle is kept separate from Piel intensive kindle by stem morphology."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3034","glossChirho":"ידה give thanks/confess","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Caught homonymous Qal root meaning 'shoot/throw' (Arabic ارْمُوا, Korean 쏘라) — entirely different from the Hifil thanksgiving root. Hifil give-thanks (Arabic حَمَدَ) vs Hifil confess-sin (Arabic اِعْتَرَفَ) is a real polysemy within the same stem. Hitpael confession overlaps semantically with Hifil confess but is a distinct morphological voice."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6256","glossChirho":"עֵת time","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Semantically uniform — 47 of 65 clusters are one sense. The כָּעֵת 'about this time/now' construction is semi-grammaticalized (Arabic كَالآنِ = now). The 'occasions/instances' sense caught by Spanish veces, Hindi बार, Korean 번, Swahili mara — all 'times' in frequency sense vs temporal sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G2980","glossChirho":"λαλέω speak","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Most aggressive merge in the batch — 54 of 67 clusters into one active sense. λαλέω is semantically almost perfectly uniform. Arabic تَكَلَّمَ/يَتَكَلَّمُ never varies. Only the active/passive voice distinction warrants a split (Arabic المَقولُ/قِيلَ for passive). No distinct glossolalia sense emerged — Corinthian tongue-speaking uses the same verb with the same translations."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1984b","glossChirho":"הלל praise/boast/be mad","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Caught homonymous הלל I (be mad, rage) alongside praise/boast. Binyan-based split: Piel praise vs Piel boast vs Pual passive vs Hitpael reflexive boasting. Arabic مَجْنُون (mad) vs يُسَبِّحُ (praise) cleanly separates homonyms. Korean 미치다 vs 찬양하다 likewise. 69→5 senses."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2026","glossChirho":"הרג kill/slay","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Clean morphological split: Qal active finite (kill) vs Qal participle substantival (killer/slayer) vs Qal passive participle (the slain) vs Nifal passive (be killed). 94→4 with massive collapse of inflectional variants. Arabic قَتَلَ (kill) vs قَاتِلٌ (killer) vs مَقْتُولٌ (slain) maps perfectly."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8074","glossChirho":"שׁמם be desolate/appalled","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Dual semantic field: physical desolation (land/cities) vs psychological horror (appalled). Both have causative Hifil counterparts. Arabic خَرِبَ (desolate) vs يَدْهَشُ (be appalled) is cleanest discriminator. Also caught agentive participle 'desolator' and abstract nominal use. 64→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3498","glossChirho":"יתר remain/leave over","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Nifal stative 'remain' vs Nifal participle substantival 'remnant' vs Hifil causative 'leave remaining' vs Hifil 'have abundance/exceed'. The participle-as-noun vs finite-verb distinction is lexicographically significant. Korean 남다 (remain) vs 나머지 (remnant) vs 남기다 (leave over) vs 넘치다 (exceed). 67→4."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G1325","glossChirho":"δίδωμι give","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"General give/bestow vs passive be given vs idiomatic put/place (εἰς τὴν καρδίαν = into the heart) vs yield/produce (of earth/trees) vs give up/hand over. Arabic أَعْطَى vs وُضِعَ vs أَنْتَجَ provides good discrimination. 59→5 with the put/place extension being a genuine Greek idiom."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8354","glossChirho":"שׁתה drink","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Most aggressive merge in batch: 97→3 senses. 91 clusters→1 'drink' sense covering all Qal inflections. Separated substantival participle 'drinker' (5 IDs) and single Nifal passive 'be drunk' (1 ID). Arabic شَرِبَ (drink) vs شَارِبٌ (drinker) confirms the agent-noun split."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5162","glossChirho":"נחם comfort/relent","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Rich Nifal polysemy: 'relent/repent' (Arabic نَدِمَ) vs 'be comforted' (Arabic تَعَزَّى) — same stem, completely different semantics. Hitpael 117244 classified by meaning (relent) not morphology. Compassion sense (يَتَرَحَّمُ) crosses both Nifal and Hitpael. Pual kept separate from Nifal passive per BDB/HALOT. 79→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3384b","glossChirho":"ירה teach/shoot","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Two fully homonymous roots: ירה I Hifil 'teach' (Arabic عَلَّمَ) vs ירה II Qal 'shoot/throw' (Arabic رَمَى). Teacher participle (מורה) substantivized. Caught rare 'send rain' sense (104053, 104068) — possibly from ירד root. 'Show/point/direct' is genuine extended sense of teach root. Arabic الرُّمَاة (archers) confirms substantival split. 67→8."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0241","glossChirho":"אֹזֶן ear","sensesChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Most aggressive merge in project: 52→1 sense. Pure body-part noun with zero semantic variation across all 6 languages. Metonymic 'hearing/attention' belongs to prepositional constructions (בְּאָזְנֵי), not the noun itself. Arabic أُذُن and مَسَامِع are register variants, not polysemy. Follows BDB/HALOT single-entry treatment."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2490c","glossChirho":"חלל begin/profane","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Two homonymous roots: חלל I Hifil 'begin' (Arabic ابْتَدَأَ) vs חלל II Piel 'profane' (Arabic دَنَّسَ). Caught specialized legal sense 'use fruit/treat as common' (Lev 19, Deut 20) — Arabic يُحَلِّلُهُ 'make permissible' is diagnostic. Hifil causative 'profane/break covenant' kept separate. Passive Nifal/Pual as own sense. 81→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1366","glossChirho":"גְּבוּל border/territory","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Not a pure single-sense noun like H0241. Four genuine senses: border/boundary (geographic line), territory (enclosed area), edge/rim (of altar — Hindi किनारी, Spanish borde), and ordained boundary landmark (wisdom lit./legal prohibition on moving). Arabic حَدّ (line) vs تُخوم (region) cleanly separates senses 1-2. 57→4."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G2192","glossChirho":"ἔχω have","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"91→7 senses. Core 'possess/have' absorbs ~69 clusters. Genuine splits: hold/regard (Arabic يَعتَبِرونَ), physical grasp (Arabic مُتَمَسِّكينَ), frozen participles τῇ ἐχομένῃ 'following day' and τὰς ἐχομένας 'neighboring', pregnancy idiom ἐν γαστρὶ ἔχω (Arabic حَامِلَة), copular state use (καλῶς ἔχω). All 7 match BDAG entries."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0430","glossChirho":"אֱלֹהִים God/gods","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Theologically central lemma. 2599 occ, 56→4 senses. Massive 2179-occ cluster is 'God of Israel' as expected. Clean separation: Arabic اللّٰه (God) vs آلِهَة (gods plural) vs إِلٰه (single foreign god). 'Judge/ruler' sense (Exod 21-22) not distinguishable in cluster data — absorbed into God-of-Israel. 'Divine being' (1 Sam 28:13) captured as separate sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7999a","glossChirho":"שׁלם be complete/repay","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Rich binyan-based polysemy: Piel pay/repay vs fulfill vows vs make restitution, Qal stative 'be complete/at peace', Pual passive, Hifil 'make peace' vs 'complete/finish'. Arabic و-ف-ي (fulfill) vs ج-ز-ي (repay) cleanly separates the two Piel senses. Swahili -lipa (pay) vs -timiza (fulfill) confirms. 70→7."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5771","glossChirho":"עָוֹן iniquity/guilt/punishment","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Classic BDB tripartition (sin/guilt/punishment) confirmed but highly skewed: ~86 of 93 clusters = 'iniquity/sin'. Only 2 clusters clearly = 'guilt' (Swahili hatia diagnostic), 3 = 'punishment' (Korean 벌, Swahili adhabu). Two anomalous singletons: textual variant 'affliction' and possible lemma error. 93→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5869a","glossChirho":"עַיִן eye","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Spring/fountain homonym (Korean 샘/우물) not present in this lemma's data — likely separate lemma ID. Three genuine senses: evaluative 'in the sight of' (~630 occ, dominant), literal eye (body part), and 'appearance/surface' (כְּעֵין construction, Korean 모양/모습 diagnostic). 77→3."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1961","glossChirho":"היה be/become","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Most common Hebrew verb (3576 occ). Conservative merge: copular/existential/narrative NOT split (Arabic كَانَ covers all three). Inchoative 'become' cleanly separated by Arabic صَارَ and Korean 되다. Caught unique Exod 3:14 'I AM' self-revelation sense (Arabic transliterates אֶהְיֶה rather than translating). Nifal stem separate. 97→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8478","glossChirho":"תַּחַת under/instead","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Clean three-way split: spatial 'under' (Arabic تَحْتَ, 24 clusters), substitutionary 'instead of' (Arabic بَدَلَ/عِوَضَ, 39 clusters — includes royal succession formula), causal 'because' (Arabic بِسَبَبِ, 6 clusters). Succession merged with general substitution per BDB. 69→3."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2930a","glossChirho":"טמא be unclean/defile","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Key split: Piel declarative 'pronounce unclean' (priestly, Spanish declarar impuro) vs Piel factitive 'defile/make unclean' (Spanish contaminar). Ritual vs moral defilement NOT split — Arabic نَجَّسَ used for both. Six binyan-based senses. Hitpael 'defile oneself' well-attested in food/dead-body prohibitions. 70→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1869","glossChirho":"דרך tread/bend","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"'Bend the bow' cleanly separated from 'tread' via Arabic شَدَّ/يَنْزِعُ vs دَاسَ, Korean 당기다 vs 밟다. Tread grapes NOT split from general tread — same verbal action, different object. Hifil split: 'lead/guide' (Arabic أَرْشَدَ) vs 'cause to trample/thresh' (Arabic دَرْس). Single-occ 'march/advance' (Arabic دَرَجَ). 56→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6629","glossChirho":"צֹאן flock/sheep","sensesChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Pure collective noun like H0241 (ear). 76→1. All clusters = Arabic غَنَم, Korean 양/양떼, Swahili kondoo. Metaphorical 'flock of God' (Ezek 34) is contextual, not a separate lexical sense. Third single-sense merge in project (after H0341 and H0241)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2403b","glossChirho":"חַטָּאת sin/sin-offering","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Classic BDB tripartition (sin/guilt/punishment) plus two cultic senses. 73 of 92 clusters → 'sin/transgression' (Arabic خَطِيئَة, Korean 죄). Sin-offering (Arabic ذَبِيحَة الخَطِيئَة, Korean 속죄제) is 16 clusters — clearly lexicalized, not contextual. Rare singletons: punishment (Swahili adhabu diagnostic), purification water (Num 19 red heifer), and anomalous 'shame' (Korean 수치, Swahili aibu — possible textual confusion). 92→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1350a","glossChirho":"גאל redeem","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Four distinct substantival participle uses: kinsman-redeemer (Ruth, Arabic وَلِيّ), blood-avenger (Num 35 collocation גֹּאֵל הַדָּם, Arabic وَلِيُّ الدَّمِ), divine Redeemer title (Isaiah 41-63, Arabic فَادِي + possessive), and the-redeemed-ones (passive participle, Arabic مَفْدِيُّونَ). Plus Qal active finite verb 'redeem' and Nifal passive 'be redeemed'. Rich participial polysemy typical of Hebrew verbal system. 60→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6566","glossChirho":"פרשׂ spread out","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Qal 'spread out' absorbs 39 of 51 clusters (hands, wings, nets, garments). Piel bifurcates: intensive 'spread out' vs 'scatter/disperse people' (Arabic فَرَّقَ, Korean 흩뜨리다). Nifal passive 'be scattered' is single occurrence. Isa 58:7 'break bread' (פָּרֹס) is specialized metaphorical extension — Arabic يَكْسِرَ/يُوَزِّعَ, Swahili -mega confirms food-distribution sense. 51→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7230","glossChirho":"רֹב multitude/abundance","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Abundance/multitude absorbs 43 of 56 clusters — fragmented purely by prepositional prefix, article, conjunction. 'Greatness/magnitude' separated by Spanish grandeza (not abundancia), Korean 큰 (big, not 많음), Swahili urefu (length, not wingi). Quantifier 'many/much' is adjectival use. Two verbal Qal forms caught (Qal Perf 3ms + Inf). 56→4."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0899b","glossChirho":"בֶּגֶד garment","sensesChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Fourth single-sense merge in project (after H0341, H0241, H6629). Initial concern about treachery homonym unfounded — this lemma covers only the noun. Arabic ثَوْب/ثِيَاب never خَانَ; verbal בגד 'act treacherously' is separate lemma (H0898 or H0899a). 63 clusters split entirely by suffix/prefix/number/article. 63→1."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0001","glossChirho":"אָב father","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Korean 아버지→조상 shift is diagnostic for father→ancestor split. Hindi पिता→पूर्वज/पितर confirms. 'Head of fathers' house' (בֵּית אָבוֹת) is institutional sense — Hindi पितृवंश (patriarchal lineage) distinguishes from both father and ancestor. Title of respect and metaphorical originator NOT split from core father sense — insufficient cluster evidence. 57→3."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8055","glossChirho":"שׂמח rejoice/be glad","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Clean binyan-based split: Qal intransitive 'rejoice' (57 clusters), Piel causative 'make glad' (19), single Hifil attestation (Ps 92:5). Arabic Form II فَرَّحَ vs Form IV أَفْرَحَ confirms Piel/Hifil distinction. Adjective שָׂמֵחַ kept separate per BDB. Infinitive construct separated for syntactic reasons. 82→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6086","glossChirho":"עֵץ tree/wood","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Arabic provides sharpest discrimination: شَجَرَة (living tree) vs خَشَب (timber) vs حَطَب (firewood) vs عَصَا (stick) vs عُود (shaft) — five distinct Arabic lexemes. Swahili mti vs mbao vs kuni vs fimbo reinforces. Korean 나무 ambiguous (covers tree+wood+timber). Gallows/stake (Esther) and flax-stalk (Josh 2:6) are singletons. 86→7."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5315","glossChirho":"נֶפֶשׁ soul/life/person","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Korean is primary discriminator: 영혼 (soul) vs 생명/목숨 (life) vs 사람 (person) vs 뜻/소원 (desire/will) vs 숨결 (breath) vs 생물 (creature). Swahili nafsi vs uhai vs mtu vs tamaa vs pumzi confirms. BDB/HALOT alignment: soul/life/creature/desire/person + breath. 57 of 100 clusters = soul/inner-being (dominant sense). Appetite/desire is clean split. 100→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2233","glossChirho":"זֶרַע seed/offspring","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Arabic نَسْل vs زَرْع is sharpest discriminator for offspring vs agricultural seed. 48 of 65 clusters = offspring/descendants (dominant covenant usage). Semen not separated — embedded in agricultural cluster, cannot isolate at cluster level. Sowing-time (Gen 8:22) caught via Korean 파종 and Swahili kupanda. 65→3."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2803","glossChirho":"חשׁב think/plan/reckon/weave","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Rich cognitive polysemy. Arabic فَكَّرَ (think) vs دَبَّرَ (scheme) vs حَسَبَ (reckon) vs قَصَدَ (intend) provides 4-way split of active senses. Exodus tabernacle craft sense (Arabic حائِكٍ weaver) clearly separate from abstract planning. Nifal passive absorbs both 'be counted' and 'be regarded' — Hebrew doesn't distinguish in passive. 95→7."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5060","glossChirho":"נגע touch/strike/reach","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Clean binyan mapping: Qal=touch (Arabic مَسَّ), Hifil=reach (Arabic بَلَغَ), Piel=strike (Arabic ضَرَبَ), Nifal/Pual=passive. Hifil causative 'bring upon' separated from intransitive 'reach'. Specialized Isa 5:8 'adjoin fields' is singleton extension. Prohibition 'don't touch' NOT split from general touch — same lexical sense, different pragmatics. 90→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7392","glossChirho":"רכב ride/mount","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Hifil splits into personal causative 'cause to ride' vs cargo 'load upon' — Korean 태우다 (person) vs 실다 (cargo), Swahili -pandisha vs -pakia are diagnostic. Qal participle 'rider/charioteer' substantivized per BDB. Divine epithet 'One who rides clouds' (Ps 68) absorbed into Qal participle. 55→4."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2603a","glossChirho":"חנן be gracious/show favor","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Hitpael 'implore favor' cleanly separated from Qal 'be gracious' — Arabic تَضَرَّعَ vs ارْحَمْ, Korean 간구하다 vs 은혜를 베풀다. Two distinct Piel senses: factitive 'make pleasant' (Arabic يُلَطِّفُ) vs intensive 'show compassion' (Arabic يَرحَمون). Disputed Nifal 'groan' (98082) may be separate root per BDB. 55→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7911","glossChirho":"שׁכח forget","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"49 of 66 clusters = Qal 'forget' (Arabic نَسِيَ, Korean 잊다). Nifal passive well-attested (11 occ). Piel and Hifil causative merged despite different stems — identical semantics (Arabic أَنْسَى). Single Hitpael (Eccl 8:10) kept separate — Arabic يَذْهَبُونَ suggests 'fade from memory' nuance. Participle and infinitive absolute separated per BDB. 66→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6912","glossChirho":"קבר bury","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Piel intensive/professional burial (Ezek 39 Gog mass interment) separated from Qal general burial. Piel participle הַקֹּבְרִים 'the buriers' is professional designation — Arabic الدَّافِنُون, Swahili wazikaji. Nifal/Pual/QalPass all merged as single passive sense (identical cross-linguistic profile). 56→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7227a","glossChirho":"רַב great/many/much","sensesChirho":10,"notesChirho":"Most senses of any lemma so far: 100→10. Arabic كَثِير vs عَظِيم vs رَئِيس vs كَفَى vs أَكْثَر provides 5-way split. Construct 'abounding in' is genuine adjectival sub-sense. Verbal forms (Qal 'increase') are separate POS. Title sense (chief/captain) may be homographic with Aramaic רַב. Singleton 'defender' likely from ריב root. 100→10."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6440","glossChirho":"פָּנִים face/before","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Massive grammaticalization: spatial לִפְנֵי (Arabic أَمَامَ) vs causal מִפְּנֵי (Arabic بِسَبَبِ) vs temporal לְפָנִים (Arabic قَبْلَ) — three completely different Arabic/Korean/Swahili words from same Hebrew noun. Cluster 127821 alone has ~1728 occurrences (spatial 'before'). Surface/face-of-earth kept separate (Korean 표면). 65→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4191","glossChirho":"מות die/kill/death","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Clean voice/causativity split: Qal die (Arabic مَاتَ) vs Hifil kill (Arabic قَتَلَ/أَمَاتَ) vs Hofal be-executed (Arabic يُقْتَلُ). מוֹת יוּמָת formula kept separate — emphatic legal/divine construction. Nominal participle 'the dead' (Arabic مَيِّت) and infinitive-as-noun 'death' (Arabic مَوْت) both substantivized. Piel/Polel intensive kill = 2 occ only. 89→7."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G1097","glossChirho":"γινώσκω know","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Could not reliably split experiential عَرَفَ from intellectual عَلِمَ — translators used both Arabic forms inconsistently within same clusters. 65 of 83 clusters = general 'know'. Passive 'be known' (8 clusters) cleanly separated by Arabic مَعْروف/عُرِفَ, Korean 알려지다. 'Understand' separated by Arabic فَهِمَ, Korean 깨닫다. Sexual 'know carnally' is singleton (Matt 1:25). 83→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3513","glossChirho":"כבד be heavy/honor/glory","sensesChirho":12,"notesChirho":"Most senses tied with H6965b (12). Arabic كَرَّمَ (honor person) vs مَجَّدَ (glorify God) is diagnostic Piel split — Korean 공경하다 vs 영화롭게 하다 confirms. Hifil 'harden heart' (Arabic أَثْقَلَ) vs Piel/Qal 'harden heart' kept separate by stem. Wealthy sense (Arabic غَنِيّ, Korean 부요했다) is genuine extension. Hitpael reflexive 'boast/multiply oneself' well-attested (3 occ). 92→12."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7604","glossChirho":"שׁאר remain/be left","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Clean Nifal/Hifil split: Arabic بَقِيَ (remain) vs أَبْقَى (leave remaining), Korean 남다 vs 남기다. Nifal participle 'remnant/survivors' substantivized per BDB — theologically significant prophetic term. 21 of 75 clusters = participle. All three senses have clean cross-linguistic separation. 75→3."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G0191","glossChirho":"ἀκούω hear/listen/obey","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"69 of 97 clusters = basic 'hear' (Arabic سَمِعَ). Swahili -sikiliza vs -sikia separates 'listen attentively' from 'hear'. Imperative/exhortatory 'heed/obey' (Korean 들으라) includes Synoptic 'he who has ears' formula. Passive 'be heard' (Arabic سُمِعَ, Korean 들리다, Swahili -sikika) is 7 clusters. Experiential→factual know split NOT attempted (unlike G1097). 97→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1870","glossChirho":"דֶּרֶךְ way/road/journey","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"56 of 82 clusters = metaphorical 'way of life/conduct' — dominant sense by cluster count. Swahili safari vs njia vs upande wa cleanly separates journey/road/direction. Arabic مَسِيرَة (journey-distance) vs طَرِيق (road) vs عَادَة (custom) provides 3-way split. Korean 길 is unfortunately ambiguous across most senses. Singleton 'direction' (Arabic نَحْوَ). 82→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0905","glossChirho":"בַּד alone/pole/linen","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Multiple homonymous roots collapsed under one lemma ID. Arabic وَحْدَهُ (alone) vs عصوين (staves) vs أَعْضَاء (limbs) vs فُرُوع (branches) vs قُضْبَان (bars) — 5 distinct Arabic lexemes. 'Apart from' (מִלְּבַד) is grammaticalized prepositional compound. Bars/bolts distinguished from poles/staves by Arabic مزاليج vs عصوين, Korean 빗장 vs 채. Linen sense NOT present in this data. 52→7."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6605a","glossChirho":"פתח open","sensesChirho":10,"notesChirho":"Arabic فَتَحَ (open) vs حَلَّ (loose) vs سَلَّ (draw sword) is primary 3-way split. Piel 'loose/untie' and 'set free person' are distinct extensions — Arabic فَكَّ/أَطْلَقَ, Korean 풀다/놓아주다. Open mouth/eyes separated from physical open per BDB. Draw sword (Arabic سَلَّ, Korean 빼다) is specialized military sense. Hitpael reflexive 'loose oneself' well-attested. 84→10."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2654a","glossChirho":"חפץ delight/desire","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Clean 2-way split: emotional pleasure (Arabic سُرَّ/رَضِيَ, Korean 기뻐하다) vs volitional desire (Arabic رَغِبَ/أُريدُ, Korean 원하다). Potential 3rd sense 'be willing/consent' (Arabic شَاءَ) collapsed into desire per BDB/HALOT. Most aggressive verb merge in recent batches: 53→2. Participial/adjectival absorbed into delight sense. 53→2."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5288","glossChirho":"נַעַר boy/youth/servant","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Arabic صَبِيٌّ (child) vs فَتًى/شَابّ (youth) vs غُلَام+possessive (servant) provides 3-way age/role split. Korean 아이 vs 젊은이 vs 종/하인 confirms. Temporal 'from youth' (מִנַּעַר) is grammaticalized expression. Armor-bearer and household attendant absorbed into servant sense. Feminine נַעֲרָה not in this lemma ID. 89→4."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7307","glossChirho":"רוּחַ spirit/wind/breath","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic رُوح (spirit) vs رِيح (wind) — completely different Arabic words from different roots — is the most powerful discriminator. Swahili roho/upepo/pumzi provides independent 3-way confirmation. Korean 영/바람/숨 also confirms. Human spirit (27 clusters) vs divine Spirit (15) distinguished by context + Swahili capitalization. Spirit beings (أرواح broken plural) and direction (Arabic جِهَة, Korean 방향) are clean singletons. 84→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3027","glossChirho":"יָד hand/power/side","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"75 of 87 clusters = literal hand — massive inflectional fragmentation. Power/authority sense under-separated (only 1 cluster explicit; many power uses absorbed into literal hand — future token-level disambiguation needed). Side/bank (Arabic جَانِب) and monument (Hindi स्थान) are genuine BDB senses. Tabernacle tenon/peg (Hindi चूलें, Spanish espigas) is technical construction term. Idiomatic כְּיַד 'according to means' caught. 87→7."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6999a","glossChirho":"קטר burn incense/make smoke","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Piel 'burn incense' (Arabic بَخَّرَ) vs Hifil 'burn sacrifice' (Arabic أَوْقَدَ) is primary split — but Hifil also has incense-burning uses (Kings/Chronicles high places), kept as separate sense 3. SoS 3:6 Pual 'be perfumed' is genuine metaphorical extension. Single Hofal passive. 52→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5158a","glossChirho":"נַחַל wadi/brook/torrent","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Arabic وَادٍ (wadi) vs نَهْر (river) vs سَيْل (torrent) provides clean 3-way split. Valley/ravine (Korean 골짜기, Swahili bonde) separated from watercourse sense. Mining-shaft sense (Job 28:4) noted but cannot be isolated at cluster level — single occurrence mixed into wadi cluster. 63→4."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G2309","glossChirho":"θέλω want/wish/will","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Arabic أَرَادَ (want) vs شَاءَ (will/sovereign purpose) is key volitional split. 'Be willing/consent' (conative) separated from active 'want'. Optative/counterfactual 'wish' identified by Arabic كُنتُ أُريدُ (past continuous unfulfilled), Spanish subjunctive quisiera. 'Delight in' (Arabic الرّاغِبُ) is affective extension. 32 of 54 clusters = core 'want'. 54→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8269","glossChirho":"שַׂר prince/chief/official","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"48 of 76 clusters = broad 'prince/noble/official' sense. Military 'captain/commander' separated by Arabic قَائِد, Hindi सेनापति, Korean 지휘관. Construct 'chief-of-X' (cupbearer, baker, guard) is genuine pattern. Ruler/governor (Korean 통치자, 목백) separated from general prince. Angelic prince (Daniel) not distinguishable at cluster level. 76→4."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3201","glossChirho":"יכל be able/prevail","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Clean 3-way split matching BDB: ability (Arabic اسْتَطَاعَ, Korean 할 수 있다), prevail (Arabic غَلَبَ, Korean 이기다), endure (Arabic احْتَمَلَ, Korean 참다). 59 of 79 clusters = basic modal 'can/be able'. All three senses have strong cross-linguistic separation across all 6 target languages. 79→3."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7993","glossChirho":"שׁלך cast/throw/hurl","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Korean is key discriminator: 던지다 (throw) vs 버리다 (discard/reject) vs 내던지다 (fling down) vs 내쫓다 (expel). Hofal passive (12 clusters) cleanly separated by Arabic مَطْرُوح and Korean 던져지다. 'Cast away' metaphorical rejection (Ps 51:11) is genuine theological sense distinct from physical throwing. 84→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G0906","glossChirho":"βάλλω throw/cast/put","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Arabic وَضَعَ (put/place) vs أَلْقَى (cast/throw) is primary split — Korean 넣다 vs 던지다 confirms. 'Lie sick/prostrate' (Arabic مَطروح, Korean 누워 있다) is stative, not result of violent projection. 'Pour' (Arabic سَكَبَ, Korean 붓다) distinguished by liquid medium. Wind 'strike/beat' and 'cast out/expel' are singletons. 57→7."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8193","glossChirho":"שָׂפָה lip/edge/language","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Arabic شَفَة vs لُغَة vs حَافَة vs شَاطِئ vs الكَلَام — five distinct Arabic words. Korean 입술/언어/가장자리/해변/말 confirms 5-way split. Edge-of-object (manufactured) vs shore-of-water (natural) distinguished by Arabic حَافَة vs شَاطِئ. Speech sense is rare singleton. Gen 11 language sense is metonymic. 70→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0410","glossChirho":"אֵל God/god/power","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Arabic اللّٰه (definite, YHWH) vs إِلٰه (indefinite, generic god) vs قُوَّة (power/might) provides clean 3-way core split. Korean 하나님 vs 신 vs 힘 confirms. Compound divine names (El Shaddai etc.) absorbed into God-of-Israel. Oak/terebinth homonym caught (Spanish encinas, Korean 상수리나무들) — single cluster. El-in-proper-names distinguished by transliteration pattern. 53→5."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3947","glossChirho":"לקח take/receive/fetch","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"66 of 83 clusters = core 'take/seize' (Arabic أَخَذَ). 'Receive' (Arabic قَبُول, Korean 받다) vs 'fetch' (Arabic أَحْضِرْ, Korean 가져오다) are genuine BDB sub-senses. Gen 3:24 Hitpael 'flash/flame' (Arabic مُتَّقِدَة) is semantically unrelated homonym. Nifal/QalPass 'be taken' cleanly passive. 'Take away' singleton. 83→6."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5127","glossChirho":"נוס flee/escape","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"48 of 62 clusters = core Qal 'flee' (Arabic هَرَبَ). Substantival participle 'fugitive' (Arabic هَارِب, Spanish fugitivo) is 7 clusters. Hifil splits: causative 'put to flight' (Arabic أَهْرَبَ) vs 'hide/smuggle' (Arabic يُهَرِّبَ). Rare Hitpael 'rally/be lifted up' likely denominative from נֵס (banner) — all 6 languages render as 'lift up' not 'flee'. Metaphorical 'vanish' singleton. 62→7."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0251","glossChirho":"אָח brother/kinsman","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Most aggressive merge of a kinship term: 85→2. Arabic إِخْوَان (brethren/fellows) vs إِخْوَة (blood brothers) was ONLY cross-linguistic signal for broader 'kinsman' sense. Korean/Swahili did not differentiate. 73 of 85 clusters = literal brother. Reciprocal 'fellow/another' and 'political ally' senses not distinguishable at cluster level — would need contextual disambiguation. 85→2."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3925","glossChirho":"למד learn/teach","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Textbook binyan split: Qal learn vs Piel teach vs Pual trained/passive. Arabic تَعَلَّمَ (Form V middle) vs عَلَّمَ (Form II causative) mirrors the Hebrew Qal/Piel distinction perfectly — rare morphological isomorphism across Semitic families. Korean 배우다 vs 가르치다 confirms clean intransitive/transitive split. The substantivized Piel participle 'teacher' merits a separate sense due to nominal syntax with articles and suffixes."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5066","glossChirho":"נגשׁ draw near","sensesChirho":9,"notesChirho":"Full binyan-spectrum lemma with 5 stems attested: Qal (approach), Nifal (present oneself), Hifil (bring near/offer), Hofal (be offered), Hitpael (draw near together). Arabic قَرَّبَ (bring near) vs قَدَّمَ (present offering) distinguishes general Hifil from cultic Hifil. Korean 나아가다 (go forward) vs 다가가다 (approach) captures the advance vs approach distinction within Qal. The Nifal 'overtake' extension (curses/blessings reaching someone) is semantically distinct from spatial approach."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6963a","glossChirho":"קוֹל voice/sound","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Noun rather than verb, so semantic distinctions arise from referent rather than morphology. Arabic صَوْت covers all senses uniformly (human/divine/non-vocal), but Korean provides the key discriminator: 목소리 (human voice) vs 음성 (divine voice/formal speech) vs 소리 (sound/noise) vs 천둥 (thunder). The שׁמע בְּקוֹל idiom ('hear in the voice of' = obey) is grammaticalized enough to warrant its own sense. Thunder as plural קוֹלוֹת is lexicalized — Arabic رُعُود (thunders) and Hindi गड़गड़ाहटें (thunderclaps) confirm it as a distinct meaning."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5265","glossChirho":"נסע pull up/set out","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Classic etymological specialization: concrete 'pull up tent stakes' → abstract 'set out on journey'. Arabic رَحَلَ (journey) vs انْتَزَعَ (pull out) cleanly separates the two Qal senses. Korean 출발하다 (depart) vs 뽑아내다 (extract) confirms. Hifil splits into causative-of-journey (cause to depart) vs causative-of-pulling (uproot/quarry), with Arabic اقْتَلَعَ (uproot) vs رَحَّلَ (cause to journey) as the discriminator. The Nifal passive 'be uprooted' is metaphorical/prophetic."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3988a","glossChirho":"מאס reject/despise","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Arabic provides a 3-way lexical split within the Qal: رَفَضَ (rafada, active refusal) vs ازْدَرى (izdara, contemptuous scorn) vs سَئِمْتُ (sa'imtu, visceral loathing/weariness). These are three completely different Arabic roots mapping to the same Hebrew verb — strong evidence for genuine polysemy. Nifal passive cleanly separated by Arabic مَرْفُوض (passive participle) and Swahili -kataliwa. Korean 거부하다 (reject) vs 멸시하다 (despise) confirms the reject/scorn boundary."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0113","glossChirho":"אָדוֹן lord/master","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Arabic الرَّبّ (ar-rabb, the Lord/God) vs سَيِّد (sayyid, human lord) is the single most diagnostic feature for the divine vs human split. Korean 주 (ju, divine) vs 주인 (ju-in, human master) confirms. Plural lords warranted a separate sense due to Arabic broken plurals سَادَة/أَسْيَاد (distinct morphology). Owner-of-servants distinguished from respectful-address by Hindi मालिक (proprietor) vs स्वामी (lord) and dominant 3ms suffix pattern ('his master' in narrative)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5324","glossChirho":"נצב stand/set up","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Outstanding Arabic root discrimination: و-ق-ف (standing/posture) vs ن-ص-ب (erecting/placing) vs ث-ب-ت (firmness/establishment) maps three Arabic roots onto one Hebrew lemma, revealing polysemy invisible in the Hebrew alone. The substantivized Nifal participle 'officer/overseer' is a lexicalized administrative title — Arabic وَكِيل (deputy) and Korean 감독관 (supervisor) confirm this isn't just 'one who stands'. Hofal passive cleanly separated."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5159","glossChirho":"נַחֲלָה inheritance","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Minimal polysemy noun — 54 clusters were entirely due to grammatical fragmentation (pronominal suffixes, prepositions, number). Only 2 senses: concrete territorial inheritance vs metaphorical covenant heritage. Arabic مِيرَاث (mirath) used uniformly; Swahili urithi likewise. Spanish herencia vs heredad provides a slight signal for the concrete/abstract split. This is a case where the clustering algorithm badly over-split a semantically simple lexeme."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2896a","glossChirho":"טוֹב good","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Adjective with genuine polysemy beyond inflectional variation. Arabic خَيْرٌ (comparative 'better') vs حَسَنٌ (absolute 'good') cleanly separates comparative from absolute quality. The substantivized noun 'goodness' (Arabic الخَيْرَ) is a genuine POS shift. Aesthetic beauty sense ('fair of appearance') distinguished by Arabic جَمِيلَاتٌ, Korean 아름다운, Spanish hermosa. The 'glad/merry heart' (לֵב טוֹב) uses Arabic verbal طاب, confirming a stative-verbal sense distinct from adjectival."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7272","glossChirho":"רֶגֶל foot","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Classic body-part polysemy. The 'times' sense (שָׁלֹשׁ רְגָלִים) is confirmed by total lexical divergence in all 5 target languages — Arabic مَرَّات, Korean 번, Swahili mara, Hindi बार, Spanish veces — none using a foot-related word. The leg vs foot distinction uses Spanish pierna vs pie and Korean 다리 vs 발. 'Pace/step' (Arabic خُطوة, Hindi गति) and 'on foot' (Hindi पैदल) are genuine metonymic extensions. 91 of 99 clusters were purely grammatical fragmentation of the literal foot sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5826","glossChirho":"עזר help","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Semantically narrow verb — the core 'help' meaning dominates. The substantivized Qal participle 'helper' merits its own sense because all target languages use nominal/agentive forms: Arabic مُعِين, Korean 돕는 자, Swahili msaidizi, Hindi सहायक. The single Qal passive participle occurrence ('the helped one') shows distinct passive morphology. Nifal and Hifil provide expected voice/valence variation. Low polysemy — 53 clusters were overwhelmingly grammatical over-splitting."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7291","glossChirho":"רדף pursue","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Full binyan spectrum with Qal/Piel/Nifal/Hifil/Pual all attested. Within Qal, Arabic provides the key 3-way split: طارد (chase physically) vs اضطهد (persecute/oppress) vs اسعَ (seek/follow after). Korean 쫓다 (chase) vs 핍박 (persecute) vs 따르다 (follow) confirms. The abstract 'seek after peace/righteousness' sense is a genuine metaphorical extension. Piel intensive 'pursue relentlessly' distinguished by Arabic مُتَتَبِّع (persistent follower)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7130","glossChirho":"קֶרֶב midst/inward part","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Clean 3-sense noun matching BDB/HALOT exactly. Arabic provides the primary discriminator: وَسَط (wasaṭ, spatial midst) vs داخِل/قَلْب/باطِن (interior/heart/hidden nature) vs أَحْشَاء (viscera/entrails). Korean 가운데 (midst) vs 속 (inside) vs 내장 (intestines) confirms independently. The spatial→psychological→anatomical continuum is a classic body-interior polysemy chain. Levitical sacrificial contexts isolate the viscera sense cleanly."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4294","glossChirho":"מַטֶּה staff/tribe","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Classic concrete→abstract polysemy: staff/rod → scepter of authority → tribal identity. Arabic سِبْط (tribe) vs عَصَا (staff) is the primary discriminator — two completely different roots. Korean 지파 (tribe) vs 지팡이 (staff) confirms. Branch/shoot sense distinguished by Korean 가지, Swahili tawi. Divination rod as specialized implements — Arabic سِهَام (arrows), Swahili mishale."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7896","glossChirho":"שׁית set/place","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"All Qal stem, so binyan criterion didn't apply. Arabic provides the key polysemy evidence: وَضَعَ (spatial placement) vs جَعَلَ (causative-transformative 'make into') vs عَيَّنَ (appointment) vs وَجَّهَ (direction) — 4 different Arabic roots for one Hebrew verb within the same stem. Korean 놓다/두다 (place) vs 만들다 (make) vs 정하다 (appoint) confirms. Rare case where a single-stem verb has genuine 6-way polysemy rather than morphological splits."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2022","glossChirho":"הַר mountain","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Low polysemy geographic noun. Korean provides the key discriminator for the hill-country sense: 산 (mountain) vs 산지 (sanji, mountainous region/highlands). Hindi similarly: पहाड़ (mountain) vs पहाड़ी (pahaadi, hilly area). Arabic جَبَل uniformly covers all senses. The singular/plural split (mountain vs mountains) is borderline — could be collapsed but Arabic جَبَل/جِبَال broken plural morphology confirms conventional treatment as related but distinct senses."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2015","glossChirho":"הפך overturn/change","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Rich polysemy verb with full binyan coverage (Qal/Nifal/Hitpael/Hofal). Arabic provides 4-way Qal sub-split: قَلَبَ (overturn/destroy) vs حَوَّلَ (transform/change) vs انصرف/التفت (turn direction) vs حَرَّفَ (pervert/distort). Korean mirrors: 뒤집다 (flip) vs 바꾸다 (change) vs 돌아서다 (turn) vs 변하다 (be changed). The Sodom-overthrow meaning (Qal overturn) vs general transformation (Qal change) is a genuine polysemy split confirmed by all 5 target languages."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7812","glossChirho":"שׁחה bow down/worship","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Overwhelmingly Hitpael (71 of 73 clusters). The agent correctly unified worship-of-God and bow-before-king as contextual rather than lexical — Arabic سَجَدَ (sajada) used uniformly in both contexts. Qal imperative (1 occ) distinguished by Arabic انْحَنِي (inhani, bend/incline) — physical posture without devotional force. Hifil causative (1 occ) = 'cause to bow down'. The Hitpael dominance makes this one of the most lopsided sense distributions in the project."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7462b","glossChirho":"רעה shepherd/tend","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"The shepherd/tend/graze/devour semantic chain is a classic valency-based polysemy. Spanish provides the clearest transitive/intransitive split: apacentar (transitive tend) vs pacer/pastar (intransitive graze). Swahili -chunga (tend) vs -kula (eat) confirms. The substantivized participle 'shepherd' (34 clusters) is cleanly nominal across all languages — Arabic رَاعِي, Korean 목자, Swahili mchungaji. The destructive 'devour' extension (3 occ) is a genuine metaphorical sense, not just strong coloring of 'feed'."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0982","glossChirho":"בטח trust","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Arabic root distinction is the key discriminator: تَوَكَّلَ/وَثِقَ (active trust in someone) vs مُطْمَئِنّ (root ط-م-ن, stative feeling of security/ease). Two completely different Arabic roots map to one Hebrew Qal, revealing the active-trust vs stative-security polysemy. The stative sense covers both positive security and negative complacency (Isaiah's rebuke) — the difference is contextual. Hifil causative 'cause to trust' (5 occ) cleanly separated by binyan."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7931","glossChirho":"שׁכן dwell","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Qal splits 4 ways: dwell/inhabit (core), settle/rest-upon (Arabic لِيَحُلَّ, different root from سَكَنَ), be-inhabited (passive), and substantivized participle 'inhabitant' (Arabic سَاكِن nominal form). The Piel and Hifil are both causative but distinguished by morphology and by their Arabic forms. The 'settle/rest upon' sense (Arabic حَلَّ = alight/descend-upon) is semantically distinct from permanent dwelling — confirmed by Spanish reposar vs habitar."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5608a","glossChirho":"ספר count/tell","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Clean binyan-based split: Qal count / Nifal be counted / Piel narrate-tell / Pual be told. The Piel 'enumerate exhaustively' sub-sense is a semantic bridge between counting and narrating — Arabic أُحْصِي (root ح-ص-ي, exhaustive enumeration) vs أَخْبَرَ/حَدَّثَ (narrative roots). Despite English glosses varying between tell/recount/declare/proclaim, Arabic and Korean evidence does NOT separate these into distinct Piel senses — they are contextual nuances."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3240","glossChirho":"ינח cause to rest/leave","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic provides a remarkable 4-way Hifil split: وَضَعَ (place/set down) vs تَرَكَ (leave/abandon) vs دَعَ (permit/let alone) vs طَرَحَ (cast down/throw) — four completely different Arabic roots for one Hebrew Hifil. The etymological core 'give rest/settle' (Arabic أَسْكَنَ/أَرَاحَ) is surprisingly rare (4 occ). Hofal passive cleanly separated. Sense 102540 correctly assigned to 'cast down' based on Arabic أُلْقِيكُمْ despite English gloss reading 'leave'."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7043","glossChirho":"קלל curse/be light","sensesChirho":12,"notesChirho":"Highest sense count this quad — 12 senses across 7 attested stems. The root q-l-l spans an extraordinary semantic range: be-light → be-swift → be-insignificant → curse (Piel intensive) → shake/agitate. Arabic provides clean splits: لعن (curse) vs خفّف (lighten burden) vs اهان (dishonor) vs رجّ (shake) vs حقير (insignificant). The Piel 'shake/agitate' (Arabic رجّ, Korean 흔들었다) is genuinely distinct from Piel 'curse' — zero translation overlap. Ties H3513 and H6965b for highest sense count."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6186a","glossChirho":"ערך arrange/set in order","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"All Qal — binyan criterion didn't apply. Arabic provides 4-way split: صَفَّ/اصْطَفَّ (battle array — Form I/VIII) vs رَتَّبَ (arrange in order — Form II) vs أَعَدَّ (prepare — Form IV) vs عادَلَ/قارَنَ (compare/equal). The compare/equal sense is a genuine semantic outlier — appears in wisdom literature (Job, Psalms, Isaiah). Psalm 23:5 'prepare a table' is cleanly separated by Arabic أَعَدَّ and Swahili -andaa/-tayarisha (prepare) rather than -panga (arrange)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3847","glossChirho":"לבשׁ clothe/put on","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Beautiful Arabic Form I/IV isomorphism with Hebrew Qal/Hifil: لَبِسَ (labisa, wear oneself) vs أَلْبَسَ (albasa, clothe someone). Korean 입다 (wear) vs 입히다 (causative clothe) mirrors exactly. Swahili kuvaa vs kuvika/kuvisha confirms. The Qal participle 'clothed in' warranted separation as a stative/adjectival sense. Pual passive 'be clothed' (2 occ) is the intensive passive counterpart."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6327a","glossChirho":"פוץ scatter","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Clean binyan split: Hifil causative scatter / Nifal passive be-scattered / Qal intransitive scatter / Hitpael shatter. The Hitpael sense 'be shattered/break apart' (Arabic تَفَتَّتَ crumble, Korean 부서지다 be broken, Swahili -vunjika be shattered) is genuinely distinct from the scatter semantic field — a different physical action entirely. Arabic Form II بَدَّدَ/شَتَّتَ (Hifil) vs Form V تَشَتَّتَ/تَفَرَّقَ (Nifal) confirms voice distinction."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3820a","glossChirho":"לֵב heart","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Massive noun (594 occ) with minimal polysemy — 72 clusters were almost entirely grammatical fragmentation. Only 3 senses: inner-self (thought/will/emotion), spatial center ('heart of the sea'), and a proper-name variant (Calebite). Korean 마음 (mind) vs 중심 (center) is the key spatial discriminator. Arabic قَلْب covers both inner-self and spatial senses (expected Semitic cognate behavior). Matches sibling lemma H3824 (לֵבָב) which also had 3 senses — cross-validation."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6908","glossChirho":"קבץ gather","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Arabic Form system maps beautifully to Hebrew binyanim: جَمَعَ (Form I = Qal simple gather) vs أَجْمَعُ (Form IV = Piel intensive gather) vs اجْتَمَعَ (Form VIII reflexive = Nifal/Hitpael assemble). Smart decision by agent to merge Nifal+Hitpael since Arabic Form VIII covers both reflexive senses — the multilingual evidence doesn't distinguish them. Pual/Qal passive 'in a gathered state' is the resultative counterpart."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3920","glossChirho":"לכד capture/trap","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Within-stem polysemy confirmed by Arabic: أَخَذَ/اسْتَوْلَى (conquer/seize) vs صَادَ/اصْطَادَ (hunt/trap) splits both Qal and Nifal. Swahili -teka (conquer) vs -nasa (trap) confirms independently. The two Hitpael occurrences have completely unrelated semantics: يَتَجَمَّدُ (freeze/congeal) vs يَتَمَاسَكُونَ (hold together/interlock) — rare case of a single stem with two genuinely unrelated senses."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0168","glossChirho":"אֹהֶל tent","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Near-monosemous noun — 76 of 77 clusters were pure grammatical fragmentation (pronominal suffixes, prepositions, construct state, number). All 5 target languages use one word uniformly: Arabic خَيْمَة, Korean 장막, Swahili hema, Hindi तम्बू, Spanish tienda. Even 'Tent of Meeting' (אֹהֶל מוֹעֵד) uses the same root — the specialized meaning comes from the qualifying noun, not polysemy. The one outlier is a textual/parsing anomaly."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G3870","glossChirho":"παρακαλέω comfort/exhort","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Rich NT Greek polysemy: entreat/exhort/comfort/be-comforted/invite. Arabic provides the best discriminators: تَوَسَّلَ (entreat) vs حَثَّ/وَعَظَ (exhort/admonish) vs عَزّى (comfort, Form II) vs تَعَزّى (be comforted, Form V) vs دَعا (invite). The Arabic active/reflexive Form II/V split (عَزّى/تَعَزّى) beautifully mirrors Greek active/passive voice for comfort. Korean 간청하다 vs 권면하다 vs 위로하다 confirms 3-way active split."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7451c","glossChirho":"רָעָה evil/bad","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Adjective/noun with genuine 5-way polysemy. Arabic provides the primary split: شَرّ (moral evil) vs مصائب/بلية (calamity/disaster) vs ضيق (trouble/distress) vs سوء (harm/injury). Korean 악 vs 재앙 vs 환난 vs 해 confirms independently. The concrete plural 'evil deeds' (Arabic شرور plural) warranted separation from the abstract quality 'wickedness'. All grammatical variations (suffixes, prepositions, article) were treated as inflectional."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7646","glossChirho":"שׂבע be satisfied","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Clean 3-sense split: Qal be-satisfied / Qal be-surfeited-weary / Hifil-Piel causative. The negative surfeited sense is diagnosed by Korean 싫증내다 (be sick of) vs 배부르다 (be full), Swahili kuchoka (tire of) vs kushiba (be satisfied), Spanish harto (fed up) vs saciado (satisfied). Smart merge of Piel+Hifil since translation signatures are indistinguishable for the causative sense. Arabic أَشْبَعَ (causative Form IV) confirms."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1368","glossChirho":"גִּבּוֹר mighty","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Low polysemy adjective/noun. Korean honorific markers distinguish the divine epithet: 강하신 (with -sin honorific) for God vs 용사 (warrior) for humans. Swahili shujaa (warrior) vs mwenye nguvu (possessor of strength) confirms. The attributive adjective sense ('strong/mighty' as quality) is borderline but supported by different Korean/Swahili lexemes from the warrior sense. 61 of 68 clusters collapsed into the single warrior sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4422","glossChirho":"מלט escape/deliver","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Clean binyan split: Nifal escape / Piel deliver / Hitpael escape-oneself / Hifil cause-to-escape. The biological 'bring forth/lay eggs' sense is a remarkable polysemy outlier — Arabic بَاضَت (laid eggs) / أَنْجَبَت (gave birth) vs نَجَّى (rescue) are completely unrelated. Korean 낳았다 (give birth) vs 구하다 (rescue) confirms. Smart decision to merge Piel+Hifil biological uses into one sense despite crossing binyanim."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G2919","glossChirho":"κρίνω judge","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Arabic provides the key active-voice polysemy split: دَانَ/حَكَمَ (judge judicially) vs قَرَّرَ (decide/determine) vs خَاصَمَ (sue/litigate). Korean 심판하다 vs 결정하다 vs 고소하다 mirrors. The 'decide/resolve' sense (11 clusters) is genuinely distinct — includes both active and passive forms since the semantic domain is consistently 'decide' not 'judge'. Greek passive correctly separated for the judicial sense. 1 Corinthians 6:1 'sue' sense is a hapax confirmed by BDAG."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3671","glossChirho":"כָּנָף wing","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Classic body-part → spatial extension polysemy. Arabic provides complete lexical divergence: جَنَاح (wing) vs أَرْكَان/أَطْرَاف (corners/extremities) vs طَرَف/ذَيْل (hem/skirt). Korean 날개 vs 끝/모서리 vs 자락/옷자락 confirms independently. The garment-skirt sense is theologically significant (Ruth 3:9 'spread your kanaf over me', David cutting Saul's kanaf). God's protective 'wings' is metaphorical but uses the same bird-wing vocabulary in all languages."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4940","glossChirho":"מִשְׁפָּחָה family/clan","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Near-monosemous noun — 61 of 62 clusters are the kinship 'clan' sense. Arabic عَشِيرَة/عَشَائِر (clan) is uniform. The single outlier 'kind/category' (Arabic أَصْنَاف kinds, Korean 종류들 types) is a genuine classificatory extension confirmed by total lexical divergence in all target languages. 304 occurrences make this one of the highest-frequency nouns processed — virtually all grammatical fragmentation."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G2983","glossChirho":"λαμβάνω receive/take","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Key Greek NT verb with genuine 6-way polysemy. Swahili provides the clearest receive/take split: pokea (receive) vs chukua (take) vs shika (seize). Korean 받다 (receive) vs 취하다 (take) vs 잡다 (seize) vs 영접하다 (welcome person) provides a 4-way active split. Arabic نال/قبل (receive) vs أخذ (take) confirms. The 'collect taxes' sense (1 occ, Swahili wakusanyaji) is a specialized commercial use. Greek passive voice correctly separated."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8610","glossChirho":"תפשׂ seize/handle","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"The handle/wield sense (Arabic عَازِفٍ player/musician, Korean 다루는 자 one who manages) is genuinely distinct from seize-by-force (Arabic أَمْسَكَ). The Qal passive 'overlaid' with metal (Arabic مُغَشّى covered) is a specialized metallurgical sense unique to this participle. Nifal passive cleanly separated. Piel intensive (Arabic Form V تَتَمَسَّكُ 'hold fast') has a sustained-grasping nuance."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4325","glossChirho":"מַיִם water","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Effectively monosemous — 583 occurrences, 53 of 54 clusters are just 'water'. All 5 target languages use one word uniformly: Arabic مَاء/مِيَاه, Korean 물, Swahili maji, Hindi पानी/जल, Spanish agua. Zero lexical divergence across any translation pair. The one outlier (sense 111274) is a ketiv/qere textual anomaly where every translation renders 'who?' — not water at all. Highest-frequency near-monosemous noun in the project."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2617a","glossChirho":"חֶסֶד lovingkindness","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Theologically central word with genuine 6-way polysemy. Arabic provides the primary split: رحمة (covenant mercy/steadfast love) vs إحسان (interpersonal kindness) vs حظوة/نعمة (favor/grace) vs محبة (devoted love). Korean 인자하심 vs 친절 vs 자비 vs 은혜 vs 사랑 confirms. The plural 'loyal acts' (Arabic مراحم plural, Korean 인자하심들) warranted separation as concrete deeds vs abstract quality. Aligns with HALOT/BDB/DCH — strong external validation."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0977","glossChirho":"בחר choose","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Good participle split: Qal passive participle 'chosen/elect' (person — Arabic مُخْتَار) vs 'choice/excellent' (quality — Arabic بَحُور, Swahili bora). These are genuinely different uses confirmed by total lexical divergence. 54 of 64 clusters collapsed into the single Qal active 'choose' sense — low polysemy verb where most over-splitting was pure grammatical fragmentation. Nifal and Pual passive stems add expected voice variants."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8313","glossChirho":"שׂרף burn","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"61→4. Clean participial analysis: resultative state (burned) and agent (one who burns) separated from finite forms. Arabic Form IV أحرق vs Form VIII تحترق confirms active/passive split. Cohortative in Gen 11:3 (brick-making) kept with active despite Korean 굽자 (bake)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G4100","glossChirho":"πιστεύω believe","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"68→4. Arabic ʾ-m-n root morphology is the key discriminator: Form I active آمَنَ (believe), Form VIII passive ائتُمِنَ (be entrusted), Form VIII reflexive يَأْتَمِنُ (entrust), ṣ-d-q root passive صُدِّقَ (be believed). Korean 믿다 vs 맡겨지다 vs 믿어지다 corroborates. JS comment line stripped from output."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G4137","glossChirho":"πληρόω fill/fulfill","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"52→5. Arabic tri-literal root system is strongest discriminator: م-ل-أ (physical filling) vs ت-م-م (prophecy fulfillment) vs ك-م-ل (completion). Active fill vs passive be-filled correctly split via Arabic Form VIII reflexive. JS comment line stripped from output."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8552","glossChirho":"תמם be complete","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"52→6. Arabic تمّ (be complete) vs فَنِيَ (perish) is the single most important discriminator between completion and destruction senses within Qal. Hitpael reflexive correctly separated for moral perfection. Hifil split into causative-complete vs causative-destroy mirrors the Qal split."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5921a","glossChirho":"עַל upon/over","sensesChirho":9,"notesChirho":"62→9. Preposition with 5,769 occurrences — most polysemous preposition so far. Arabic prepositions are strongest discriminator: عَلَى (spatial), عِنْدَ (proximity), عَنْ (topical), إِلَى (directional), بِحَسَبِ (normative), لِأَجْلِ (benefactive). Korean postpositions confirm independently: 위에/대항하여/에 대하여/옆에/를 위하여/에게."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2398","glossChirho":"חָטָא sin/miss","sensesChirho":9,"notesChirho":"91→9. Arabic root split is decisive: خ-ط-أ (sin) vs ط-ه-ر (purify) confirms Piel/Hitpael purification as genuinely different sense. Rare concrete 'miss a target' (Judg 20:16) distinguished by Korean 빗나가다. Piel 'bear the loss' (Gen 31:39) isolated as hapax commercial sense with Arabic أُكَفِّرُ."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6106","glossChirho":"עֶצֶם bone/self","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"61→3. Arabic root divergence: عِظَام (bone) vs عَيْنِ/ذَاتِ/نَفْسِ (selfsame) vs جَوهَرِ/كَمَالِ (essence). Korean 뼈 vs 바로 vs 한창 속에 confirms three-way split. 46 of 61 clusters were purely morphological variants of 'bone' (possessive suffixes, prepositions)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3225","glossChirho":"יָמִין right hand/south","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"63→3. Clean three-way lexical split across all target languages. Spanish diestra/derecha/sur is especially diagnostic. Swahili mkono wa kulia (hand) vs kulia (direction) vs kusini (south). Korean 오른손 vs 오른쪽 vs 남. The south sense (4 clusters) is genuine polysemy from Hebrew eastward orientation."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G2476","glossChirho":"ἵστημι stand/set","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"56→6. Greek tense-stem distinction is key: transitive 1st aorist ἔστησα (set/place) vs intransitive 2nd aorist ἔστην (stand). Arabic وَاقِف (positional) vs يَثْبُتُ (endure) vs أَقَامَ (causative) vs وَزَنُوا (weigh). Commercial 'weigh out' sense (Zech 11:12) isolated via Arabic وَزَنُوا."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G0863","glossChirho":"ἀφίημι forgive/leave","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"65→8. Three Arabic roots provide the primary split: تَرَكَ (leave), غَفَرَ (forgive), سَمَحَ (permit). Active/passive pairs correctly separated for both leave and forgive. 'Yield up spirit' (Matt 27:50) isolated via Arabic أَسْلَمَ. 'Neglect' separated from physical 'leave' by object type."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7378","glossChirho":"רִיב contend/plead","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"58→5. Arabic خَاصَمَ (quarrel) vs رَافَعَ (litigate) provides clean split between general strife and juridical pleading. Korean 다투다 vs 변호 confirms. Hifil participle 'adversary' correctly separated. One anomalous 'ambush' occurrence (135683) flagged as possible textual issue."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7650","glossChirho":"שׁבע swear","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"66→3. Clean Niphal/Hiphil split. Arabic causative Forms IV/X/II (أَحْلَفَ/اسْتَحْلَفَ/حَلَّفَ) independently confirm the Hiphil as causative. Swahili -apisha (causative -isha suffix) provides further confirmation. Single Qal passive participle correctly isolated."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5462","glossChirho":"סגר shut/close","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"57→7. Five Arabic roots for one Hebrew verb: أَغْلَقَ (close), خَالِصٌ (pure), حَجَزَ (quarantine), أَسْلَمَ (deliver up), حَبَسَ (imprison). The 'deliver up/hand over' sense (18 clusters) is as large as the core 'shut' sense — genuine polysemy not mere extension."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3198","glossChirho":"יכח rebuke/judge","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"53→7. Five Arabic root families: وَبَّخَ (rebuke), أَدَّبَ (discipline), حَكَمَ (judge), حَاجَّ (argue), عَيَّنَ (appoint). The 'appoint/designate' sense is semantically isolated — likely a homograph or extreme extension. JS comment line stripped from output."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5769","glossChirho":"עוֹלָם eternity","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"52→5. Four Arabic temporal roots are the key discriminator: الأَبَد (future eternity), الأَزَل (past pre-eternity), الْقِدَم (antiquity/ancient), الدُّهُور (ages/eras). This is a lexicographically important result — many translations conflate these senses. Korean 영원히 vs 옛날 vs 세대들 corroborates."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0776","glossChirho":"אֶרֶץ earth/land","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"51→4. Hindi provides cleanest three-way split: देश (country), पृथ्वी (earth/world), भूमि (ground/soil). Swahili nchi/dunia/chini similarly tripartite. Plural 'lands' correctly separated via Arabic broken plural أَرَاضِي. 36 of 51 clusters were morphological variants of the dominant 'land/territory' sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7453","glossChirho":"רֵעַ friend/neighbor","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"81→5. Arabic provides four-way root split: قَرِيب (neighbor), صَدِيق (friend), صَاحِب (companion), رَفِيق (comrade). Korean 이웃/친구/동료 confirms. 'Lover/paramour' (prophetic contexts) and 'mate/counterpart' (animal pairing) isolated as singletons."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G5087","glossChirho":"τίθημι put/place","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"61→4. Arabic وَضَعَ (place) vs جَعَلَ (appoint) vs عَزَمَ (resolve). 'Lay down one's life' correctly separated via Korean 내놓다 (release) vs 놓다 (place), Swahili -toa (give out) vs -weka (place). JS comment line stripped from output."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7919a","glossChirho":"שׂכל be wise/prosper","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"52→8. Arabic disambiguates prosper (نجح) vs prudent (فَطِنٌ) vs understand (فَهِمَ) vs contemplate (تَأَمَّلَ) vs teach (عَلَّمَ). 'Maskil' psalm genre term correctly isolated as singleton. JS comment line stripped from output."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4150","glossChirho":"מוֹעֵד appointed time","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"55→5. Arabic root diversity is key: اجتماع (meeting), ميعاد (appointed time), موسم/أعياد (festival), محفل (assembly place). 'Appointed signal' (Judg 20:38) is a fascinating singleton — every target language independently uses 'signal/sign' vocabulary."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0270","glossChirho":"אחז grasp/seize","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"61→7. Arabic أَمْسَكَ (grasp) vs أَخَذَ/اسْتَوْلَى (figurative overwhelm) vs تَمَلَّكُوا (possess land) vs مُثَبَّتَةً (fasten). 'Take possession of land' (Nifal) is strongest polysemy signal — completely different Arabic root family. Piel 'cover/conceal' is a hapax semantic outlier."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2416c","glossChirho":"חַיָּה life/beast","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"52→4. Arabic حَيَوَان (animal) vs حَيَاة (life) — same root but different nominal patterns marking genuine polysemy. Ezekiel 'living creatures' correctly separated via Korean 생물 vs 짐승. Rare 'appetite' sense isolated via Arabic جُوع (hunger)."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6437","glossChirho":"פנה turn/face","sensesChirho":10,"notesChirho":"78→10. Most polysemous verb this session. 8+ Arabic roots: الْتَفَتَ (turn), مُتَّجِهَة (facing), فَنَتْ (decline), هَيِّئُوا (prepare), أَبْعَدَ (remove), يُفْرِغُونَ (empty), أَدَارُوا (cause to turn), الزَّاوِيَةِ (corner). Three distinct Piel senses is remarkable."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"G2064","glossChirho":"ἔρχομαι come/go","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"53→6. Arabic imperative uses entirely different root تَعَالَ vs indicative جَاءَ — a lexical split at the speech-act level. 'The Coming One' messianic title separated via Korean honorific 분 and Arabic definite الآتي. Korean 오다 vs 가다 confirms come/go split."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8045","glossChirho":"שׁמד destroy","sensesChirho":2,"notesChirho":"65→2. Near-monosemous — only Hifil active (destroy) vs Nifal passive (be destroyed). Arabic أَبَادَ/أَهْلَكَ variation is translator choice, not polysemy. One of the simplest merges — almost all 65 clusters were inflectional variants of the same meaning."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0539","glossChirho":"אמן believe/be faithful","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"70→6. Classic Hebrew polysemy: Qal 'nurse/guardian' (Arabic مُرَبِّي) is entirely distinct from abstract faithfulness senses. Arabic آمَنَ (believe) vs يَثِقُ (trust) separates two Hifil senses. Niphal participle 'faithful' (أَمِين) vs Niphal finite 'be confirmed' (يَثْبُتْ) is a stative/eventive split."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3789","glossChirho":"כתב write","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"75→7. Arabic كَتَبَ (write) vs مَكْتُوب (written) vs كُتِبَ (be written) confirms Qal/passive participle/Nifal three-way split. 'Scribe' (agent participle) and 'register/enroll' (specialized extension) correctly separated. One misparse (107878) flagged as tagging error."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0369","glossChirho":"אַיִן there is not","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"66→4. Arabic لَيْسَ (existential negation) vs بِلَا (privative 'without') vs كَلَا شَيْءٍ (comparative nothingness) vs لَا (general negation) — four Arabic constructions map to four genuine senses of this particle. Korean 없다/없이/아니다 corroborates."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2342a","glossChirho":"writhe, dance, be in pain","sensesChirho":11,"notesChirho":"Highly polysemous root with 9+ Arabic roots discriminating senses: و-ج-ع (pain), ر-ع-د (tremble), م-خ-ض (labor), و-ل-د (birth), ر-ق-ص (dance), ح-ل-ل/د-و-ر (whirl), ن-ظ-ر (wait), ج-ر-ح (wound). Piel causative 'bring forth' vs Qal 'travail' clearly separated. Dance sense (רָקַד cognate) only 2 clusters."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4503","glossChirho":"offering, gift, tribute","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Clean 4-way split: grain offering (Arabic تَقْدِمَة, Korean 소제), general offering (Korean 제물), secular gift (Arabic هَدِيَّة — different root entirely), political tribute (Arabic جِزْيَة). Korean compound 소제 vs generic 제물 is remarkably precise for cultic terminology."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4910","glossChirho":"rule, govern, have dominion","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Arabic distinguishes governance (ح-ك-م hakama) from dominion (ت-س-ل-ط tasallata) from royal reign (م-ل-ك malaka). Hifil causative 'grant dominion' maps to Arabic Form II سَلَّطَ vs Qal Form V تَسَلَّطَ — mirrors Hebrew binyan system perfectly."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7704b","glossChirho":"field, open country","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three distinct Arabic roots: حَقْل (cultivated field, 49 clusters), سَهْل (open plain, 2 clusters), بَرِّيّ (wild/wilderness, 1 cluster). The 49→1 compression ratio for the dominant sense shows extreme over-splitting by grammar alone."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2706","glossChirho":"statute, decree, boundary","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Three Arabic roots discriminate: فَرِيضَة (statute/ordinance), حَدّ (boundary/limit), نَصِيب (allotted portion). The boundary sense (Job 38, Prov 8) is cosmic-order language. One ketiv variant reads 'bosom' — likely scribal error."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5117","glossChirho":"rest, settle, set down","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Four Arabic roots split Qal rest (اسْتَرَاحَ r-w-ḥ X) from Qal settle (اسْتَقَرَّ q-r-r) from Hifil give-rest (أَرَاحَ r-w-ḥ IV) from Hifil deposit (أَنْزَلَ n-z-l). Swahili confirms with -pumzika vs -pumzisha (causative -isha). One cluster is a pronoun misparse (ונחנו = 'and we')."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3190","glossChirho":"be good, do well","sensesChirho":12,"notesChirho":"Most polysemous Hifil this session: 5+ Arabic roots (ح-س-ن, ط-ي-ب, ص-ل-ح, ز-ي-ن, ر-ض-ي, س-ر-ر). Specialized senses include adorn (ز-ي-ن), play skillfully (musical), trim lamps (priestly technical), and comparative 'better than'. Swahili causative -isha beautifully separates 'make merry' (furahisha) from base."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4427a","glossChirho":"reign, be king","sensesChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Clean binyan-based split: Qal reign (Arabic مَلَكَ Form I), Hifil make king (Arabic مَلَّكَ Form II with doubled radical), Hofal be made king (Arabic مُلِكَ internal passive). Korean 왕으로 세우다 (establish as king) vs 다스리다 (rule) confirms causative distinction. 79→3 is excellent compression."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7626","glossChirho":"rod, tribe, scepter","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four Arabic roots cleanly separate: سِبْط (tribe, direct Semitic cognate), عَصَا (rod/staff), صَوْلَجَان (scepter, Persian loanword in Arabic), سِهَام (dart/projectile). Korean 지파 vs 막대기 vs 홀 is remarkably precise. The tribe→rod metonymy (chieftain's rod → the people under his rod) is a classic case."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5307","glossChirho":"fall, lie","sensesChirho":13,"notesChirho":"13 senses across Qal (6) + Hitpael (1) + Hifil (6). Arabic roots: سَقَطَ (fall), هَرَبَ (desert/defect), نَزَلَ (dismount), وَقَعَ (attack), أَسْقَطَ (cause fall), أَلْقَى (cast lots), قَطَعَ (fell trees). Korean 투항한 (defect) cleanly isolates military sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2388","glossChirho":"be strong, strengthen","sensesChirho":18,"notesChirho":"Most polysemous lemma yet: 18 senses. 8+ Arabic roots (ق-و-ي, ش-د-د, م-س-ك, ر-م-م, ق-س-و, غ-ل-ب, ض-غ-ط, ث-ب-ت). Piel 'repair buildings' (Arabic رَمَّمَ) completely distinct from 'strengthen people'. Hifil seize (أَمْسَكَ) vs hold fast (تَمَسَّكَ) shows Arabic Form IV/V distinguishing sub-senses."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3318","glossChirho":"go out, bring out","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Clean Qal/Hifil/Hofal split. Arabic root خ-ر-ج throughout except solar sense which uses ط-ل-ع (rise). Swahili -toka (Qal) vs -toa (Hifil) vs -tolewa (Hofal passive) perfectly mirrors Hebrew binyan system."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3205","glossChirho":"bear, beget","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Piel 'midwife' (Arabic قَابِلَة, entirely different root) and Hitpael 'register by genealogy' (Arabic ن-س-ب) are genuine semantic shifts. Korean 낳다 (bear) vs 태어나다 (be born) is the clearest active/passive split. Swahili -zaa/-zaliwa/-zalisha shows full Bantu derivational chain."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H8085","glossChirho":"hear, listen, obey","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Within Qal: hear (سَمِعَ), listen/heed (اسْمَعْ imperative attention), obey (Spanish obedecer uniquely disambiguates). Swahili -sikia/-sikiliza/-sikiwa/-sikizisha/-tii maps 5 of 7 senses perfectly via derivational morphology."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4390","glossChirho":"fill, be full","sensesChirho":11,"notesChirho":"5 Arabic roots: م-ل-أ (fill), ت-م-م (fulfill/complete), ت-ب-ع (follow fully), ر-ص-ع (inlay gems), ش-ب-ع (satisfy appetite). Piel idiom מִלֵּא אַחֲרֵי 'follow fully' confirmed by Arabic اِتَّبَعَ. Swahili -jaa/-jaza/-jazwa chain mirrors Qal/Piel/Niphal perfectly."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3615","glossChirho":"finish, consume","sensesChirho":11,"notesChirho":"Core Piel split: أَكْمَلَ (complete task) vs أَفْنَى (destroy/annihilate). Qal intransitive فَنِيَ (come to end) vs كَلَّتْ (languish/fail). Rare senses: يَأْكُلُ (devour), وَتاقَت (yearn), قُضِيَتْ (be decreed). Korean 마쳤다 vs 멸하다 vs 사모했다 covers the range cleanly."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7126","glossChirho":"draw near, approach, offer","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Hifil split into cultic 'offer sacrifice' (Korean 드리다/바치다 reverential verbs) vs physical 'bring near' (Korean 가져오다). Arabic stative قَرُبَ vs active اقترب distinguishes 'be near' from 'approach'. Hindi चढ़ाना uniquely marks sacrificial sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3772","glossChirho":"cut, cut off, make covenant","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Covenant sense confirmed by Hindi बाँधा (bind) and Korean 맺었다 (establish) — completely different from cutting vocabulary. Arabic abandons قَطَعَ for أَبَادَ/أَفْنَى in Hifil destructive sense. Hofal rare (3 occ) but confirmed by Arabic passive participle مَقْطُوعَة."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5647","glossChirho":"serve, work, worship","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Four Arabic roots split Qal alone: ع-ب-د (worship), خ-د-م (serve person), ع-م-ل (work/labor), ف-ل-ح (till land). Swahili -abudu/-tumikia/-fanya kazi/-lima maps 4 core senses via different lexemes. Causative chain: -tumikia → -tumikisha mirrors Qal → Hifil."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6485a","glossChirho":"visit, appoint, number","sensesChirho":15,"notesChirho":"Most polysemous noun-verb this batch: 15 senses. Arabic roots عَدَّ (count), افتقد (visit), عاقب (punish), أوصى (charge), طلب (seek), أَوْدَعَ (entrust) split Qal alone into 7 senses. The 'visit' → 'punish' semantic shift is a classic Semitic phenomenon — Arabic disambiguates what Hebrew conflates."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3559a","glossChirho":"establish, prepare","sensesChirho":12,"notesChirho":"12 senses across 5 binyanim. Arabic أَعَدَّ (prepare) vs ثَبَّتَ (establish) vs صَوَابًا (right/correct) shows 3 semantic fields within Nifal alone. Rare 'direct/aim' sense uses Arabic تُصَوِّبُ — a genuine semantic extension from 'setting' to 'directing'."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7121","glossChirho":"call, proclaim, read","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Arabic provides 4 distinct roots: دَعَا (call/name), صَرَخَ (cry in distress), نَادَى (proclaim publicly), قَرَأَ (read/recite). The قَرَأَ root is the direct Arabic cognate of Hebrew קרא but narrowed to reading only. Korean 부르다/외치다/선포하다/읽다 maps perfectly."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H4941","glossChirho":"judgment, justice, ordinance","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Arabic عَدْل (justice) vs حُكْم (judgment) vs دَعْوَى (lawsuit) vs عَادَة (custom) vs حَقّ (right/entitlement) — 5 Arabic roots for one Hebrew noun. The plural أَحْكَام 'ordinances' is kept separate from singular 'ordinance' because of collective vs specific usage."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1129","glossChirho":"build, construct","sensesChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Clean 4-way split: Qal active (بَنَى), Qal participle as agent noun 'builder' (الْبَانُونَ), Qal passive participle 'built' (مَبْنِيَّة), Nifal passive 'be built' (بُنِيَتْ). Metaphorical 'be built up' (Rachel Gen 30:3) stays in Nifal. 141→4 is excellent compression."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7235a","glossChirho":"multiply, increase","sensesChirho":9,"notesChirho":"Arabic Form I كَثُرَ (intransitive) vs Form II/IV causative. Piel 'rear children' (Arabic رَبَّ) is completely distinct from numerical multiplication. Hifil InfAbs as adverb 'exceedingly' kept separate — functions syntactically as modifier not verb."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1288","glossChirho":"bless, kneel","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Euphemistic Piel 'curse/blaspheme' (Arabic جَدَّفَ) is the famous semantic reversal — all 5 occurrences confirmed by Korean 저주하다 and Swahili -laani. Qal 'kneel' (Arabic رَكَعَ) entirely different from Piel 'bless' (Arabic بَارَكَ). Arabic Form V reflexive تَبَارَكَ confirms Hitpael."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5186","glossChirho":"stretch out, incline, turn","sensesChirho":10,"notesChirho":"6 Arabic roots: مَدَّ (stretch hand), نَصَبَ (pitch tent), نَشَرَ (spread heavens), مَالَ (incline/turn), أَمَالَ (cause to incline), حَرَّفَ (pervert justice). The 'pervert justice' Hifil (Arabic حَرَّفَ) is a genuine moral-semantic extension, not just physical turning."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5800a","glossChirho":"forsake, abandon, help","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"The rare 'help with a burden' sense (Ex 23:5) confirmed by Korean 돕다 and Swahili kusaidia — a famous lexicographic crux. Main split: forsake (Arabic تَرَكَ/هَجَرَ) vs leave neutrally (Korean 남기다 'leave behind' vs 버리다 'discard'). Passive participle 'forsaken one' kept separate from eventive Nifal."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5927","glossChirho":"go up, ascend, offer","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Qal split: صَعِدَ (volitional ascent) vs طَلَعَ (natural rising — sun, plants). Hifil 4-way: sacrifice (Korean 드리다), lead people (Korean 데려가다), kindle lamps (Korean 켜다), chew cud (Arabic الْمُجْتَرَّاتِ zoological). Korean verbs are remarkably specific."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7200","glossChirho":"see, perceive","sensesChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Clean binyan split: Qal رَأَى (see), Nifal ظَهَرَ (appear — different Arabic root!), Hifil أَرَى Form IV (show), Hofal أُرِيَ (be shown). Qal 'seer/prophet' (روئي) kept separate as substantivized participle. Providential 'see to it' (Gen 22) is a genuine sub-sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H7971","glossChirho":"send, stretch out","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Qal 'stretch out hand' (Arabic مَدَّ) completely distinct from Qal/Piel 'send' (Arabic أَرْسَلَ). Piel splits 3 ways: send (أَرْسَلَ), dismiss/release (أَطْلَقَ/صَرَفَ), expel (أَخْرَجَ/طَرَدَ). 'Set fire' (Arabic أَشْعَلَ) is a rare specialized sense."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0398","glossChirho":"eat, consume, devour","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Korean 먹다 (eat) vs 삼키다 (devour) and Swahili -kula vs -teketeza (consume by fire) cleanly split literal eating from destructive devouring. Hifil 'feed' uses Arabic أَطْعَمَ Form IV — a completely different Arabic root from أَكَلَ."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3423","glossChirho":"possess, inherit, dispossess","sensesChirho":9,"notesChirho":"Hifil has two opposite meanings: dispossess/drive out (Arabic طَرَدَ) AND cause to inherit (Arabic أَوْرَثَ). Korean 차지하다 (possess) vs 상속하다 (inherit) vs 쫓아내다 (drive out) vs 가난해지다 (become poor) — 4-way split. Nifal 'become poor' (Arabic يَفْتَقِرُ) is a genuine semantic shift."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H1540","glossChirho":"uncover, exile","sensesChirho":10,"notesChirho":"Two completely separate semantic domains under one root: كشف (reveal/uncover) vs جلا/سبى (exile/deport). Piel splits into physical 'strip bare' (Korean 벗기다) vs abstract 'reveal information' (Korean 드러내다). 10 senses across 6 binyanim — well-organized polysemy."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H5414","glossChirho":"give, put, make","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"5 Arabic roots for one Hebrew verb: أَعْطَى (give), وَضَعَ (put/place), جَعَلَ (make/render), سَلَّمَ (deliver), يَدَعُ (allow/permit). Arabic preserves semantic distinctions Hebrew collapses under נתן. Korean 허락하다 vs 놓다 vs 만들다 vs 넘겨주다 confirms 4-way split."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H0622","glossChirho":"gather, collect, remove","sensesChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Likely homographic root: אסף I 'gather' (Arabic جَمَعَ) vs אסף II 'heal' (Arabic يَشْفِي). Death euphemism 'gathered to fathers' (Arabic انْضَمَّ) is semantically distinct. Piel participle 'rear guard' (Arabic الساقَةُ) is a specialized military term. 'Remove' (Arabic نَزَعَ) may be yet another root."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H2142","glossChirho":"remember, mention","sensesChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Hifil splits 3 ways: remind (Arabic أُذَكِّرُ causative), mention/invoke (Swahili taja — different verb from kumbuka), court recorder title (Korean 기록관). Swahili kumbuka/kumbusha (causative -sha) mirrors Qal/Hifil perfectly."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H3254","glossChirho":"add, continue, do again","sensesChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Core semantic split: 'add quantity' (Arabic زَادَ) vs 'continue/resume' (Arabic عَادَ — different root!). Extremely common as auxiliary verb 'do X again'. Korean 더하다 (add) vs 계속하다 (continue) confirms. Nifal 'be joined' (Arabic انضمّ) is a genuine passive extension."}
{"lemmaIdChirho":"H6310","glossChirho":"mouth, edge, opening","sensesChirho":9,"notesChirho":"9 senses for a noun — most polysemous noun this session. Arabic حَدّ (sword edge), حَسَب (according to), أَمْر (command), فَتْحَة (opening), طَرَف (end/extremity) each use entirely different Arabic words. 'Pim' weight measure is a distinct lexeme sharing consonants."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G1096","labelChirho":"γίνομαι become","beforeChirho":124,"afterChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Middle deponent verb with wide semantic range: 'become/happen' (core), 'be born' (origin), 'come into being' (creation), 'arrive/come' (motion), 'be done/happen' (event), 'prove to be' (evaluation), 'be made/become' (passive/middle), 'let it be' (imperative/optative). Arabic distinguishes صار (become) vs حدث (happen) vs وُلد (be born)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3372","labelChirho":"ירא fear","beforeChirho":124,"afterChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Root ירא cleanly splits: Qal 'fear/be afraid' vs 'revere/fear God' (emotional fear vs religious awe); Niphal 'be feared/awesome'; Piel 'frighten'; substantive 'fearful one/awesome thing'; nominal 'fear/reverence'. Arabic خاف (fear) vs هاب (revere) confirms the core split."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2421","labelChirho":"חיה live","beforeChirho":123,"afterChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Root חיה has clear binyan splits: Qal 'live/be alive' (stative) vs 'survive/remain alive' (dynamic); Piel 'preserve alive/let live' (causative of survival); Hifil 'revive/restore to life'; nominal 'living creature/animal'; adjectival 'alive/living'; temporal 'lifetime/life span'. Arabic عاش (live) vs أحيا (revive Hifil) vs حيوان (animal)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1245","labelChirho":"בקשׁ seek","beforeChirho":122,"afterChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Root בקשׁ (Piel intensive) splits: 'seek/search for' (general), 'seek to do' (attempt/intention), 'request/ask for' (petition), 'require/demand' (judicial), Pual passive 'be sought'. Arabic بحث (search) vs طلب (request) vs حاول (attempt) as key discriminators."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3627","labelChirho":"כלי vessel","beforeChirho":122,"afterChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Nominal כלי with wide semantic range: 'vessel/container', 'weapon/implement of war', 'instrument/tool', 'equipment/gear', 'jewelry/ornament', 'article/object' (generic), 'baggage/belongings'. Arabic distinguishes إناء (vessel) vs سلاح (weapon) vs آلة (instrument) vs أداة (tool)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7311a","labelChirho":"רום exalt/be high","beforeChirho":141,"afterChirho":11,"notesChirho":"Root רום splits across binyanim: Qal stative 'be high/tall' vs dynamic 'be exalted' vs divine predication vs pejorative 'haughty'; Hifil physical 'lift up' vs figurative 'exalt' vs 'contribute offering' (Arabic قدّم) vs 'remove' (Arabic أزال); Piel intensive 'extol'; Hitpael reflexive; Pual/Hofal passive. Arabic مرتفع vs متعالٍ vs متكبّر as key discriminators."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H8199","labelChirho":"שׁפט judge","beforeChirho":122,"afterChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Root שׁפט: Qal core 'judge/adjudicate', participle substantive 'judge (office)', benefactive 'vindicate/do justice for', Niphal 'enter into judgment/contend' (reciprocal), Niphal passive 'be judged', extended 'rule/govern' (Judges period). Arabic قضى vs حكم; Korean 심판하다 vs 다스리다 (judge vs rule)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5337","labelChirho":"נצל deliver","beforeChirho":121,"afterChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Root נצל: dominant Hifil 'deliver/rescue' (Arabic أنقذ/نجّى, 99 clusters), distinct Hifil 'snatch away' (Arabic انتزع, forcible removal), Niphal 'be delivered/escape', Hofal passive 'be snatched out', Piel 'strip/plunder/despoil' (completely different domain), Hitpael reflexive 'strip oneself'."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0995","labelChirho":"בין understand","beforeChirho":119,"afterChirho":9,"notesChirho":"Root בין splits by binyan: Qal 'understand/perceive' (فهم) vs 'give attention/regard' (ينتبه); Niphal ptc 'discerning/prudent' (فهيم); Hifil stative 'have understanding' (فاهم) vs causative 'teach/cause to understand' (فهّم); Hitpael 'consider/reflect' (تأمّل); Piel 'attend to/care for'. Arabic Form I vs II vs V as key discriminators."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7218a","labelChirho":"ראשׁ head","beforeChirho":118,"afterChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Nominal ראשׁ: 'head (body part)' (رأس), 'top/summit' (Korean 꼭대기), 'chief/leader' (رئيس), 'beginning/first' (أوّل), 'company/division' (فرق, military), 'sum/total/census' (jumla), 'chief/choicest/principal amount'. Arabic رأس vs رئيس vs أوّل as key discriminators."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1696","labelChirho":"דבר speak","beforeChirho":116,"afterChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Root דבר I vs דבר II clearly separated: Piel 'speak' (Arabic تكلّم, 103 clusters) dominates; Qal ptc 'speaker' (المتكلّم); Niphal reciprocal 'converse'; Hitpael reflexive 'muse'; Pual passive 'be spoken'. דבר II Hifil 'subdue' (Arabic يُخضع) and Piel 'destroy' (Arabic أبادت) are entirely different roots confirmed by Arabic."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1875","labelChirho":"דרשׁ seek/inquire","beforeChirho":116,"afterChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Root דרשׁ splits: Qal 'seek' (طلب) vs 'inquire of God' (سأل) vs 'investigate' (بحث/فحص) vs 'require/demand' (أُطالب) vs 'care for' (يعتني); Niphal 'be inquired of' vs 'be required' (مطلوب). Arabic cognate variation is the primary discriminator across all 7 senses."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6680","labelChirho":"צוה command","beforeChirho":116,"afterChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Root צוה: dominant Piel 'command' (Arabic أمر/أوصى, 107 clusters absorbed as inflectional variants); distinct 'appoint/commission' (Arabic أقام/عيّن); substantive ptc 'commander' (أميراً); contextual 'send word/dispatch orders' (أرسل); Pual passive 'be commanded' (أُمر). Highly uniform root — most splits were purely grammatical."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0006","labelChirho":"אבד perish","beforeChirho":115,"afterChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Root אבד: Qal 'perish/be destroyed' (هلك/فني) vs 'be lost/go astray' (ضاع/ضائع — completely different Arabic root confirms distinct sense); Piel 'destroy/annihilate' (أهلك/أباد); Hifil causative 'cause to perish' (أُبيد); Piel sub-sense 'waste/squander' (يُضيع). Arabic ضاع vs هلك is the key split within Qal."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3212","labelChirho":"הלך walk/go","beforeChirho":115,"afterChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Root הלך: Qal 'go/depart/travel' (ذهب) vs 'walk in/follow a way' (سلك/يمشي — moral conduct); imperative 'come!' (تعال/هلمّ — idiomatic inversion); Hifil causative 'lead/bring/cause to go' (ساق/قاد); marginal 'flow away/depart permanently' (تسيل). Arabic ذهب vs سلك vs تعال as key discriminators."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0157","labelChirho":"אהב love","beforeChirho":114,"afterChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Root אהב: Qal active 'love' (أحبّ, 65 clusters); Qal ptc substantive 'lover/friend' (محبّ, 33 clusters); Piel ptc 'paramour/illicit lover' (عشّاق, prophetic contexts); passive ptc 'beloved' (محبوب); abstract noun אהבה 'love' (محبّة); textual variant 'give' (sense 80278 = הבו imperative, homographic confusion)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0935","labelChirho":"בוא come/enter","beforeChirho":114,"afterChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Root בוא: Qal 'come/arrive' (جاء/أتى) vs 'enter/go in' (دخل — different Arabic root confirms genuine split); Hifil causative 'bring' (أحضر); Hofal passive 'be brought' (مجلوبة). Very aggressive merge — 114→4 — reflecting that most splits were purely inflectional."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5437","labelChirho":"סבב surround/turn","beforeChirho":114,"afterChirho":9,"notesChirho":"Root סבב: Qal splits 3 ways — 'turn/change direction' (دار/التفت) vs 'surround/encompass' (أحاط) vs 'go around/traverse' (طاف); Niphal 'turn/wind'; Piel 'surround' (intensive); Hifil 'cause to turn/divert' (حوّل) vs 'bring around/transfer' (نقل); Hofal 'be turned'; rare Qal 'change/transform' (تحوّل). Arabic root variation is primary discriminator within Qal."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6213a","labelChirho":"עשׂה do/make","beforeChirho":113,"afterChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Root עשׂה: Qal 'do/perform' (فعل, 65 clusters) vs 'make/construct' (صنع, 31 clusters — different Arabic root) vs 'work/labor' (عمل); Niphal 'be done/made'; agricultural 'produce/yield' (ينتج); substantival ptc 'Maker/Creator' (صانع). Arabic فعل vs صنع vs عمل as primary 3-way discriminator."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7665","labelChirho":"שׁבר break","beforeChirho":113,"afterChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Root שׁבר: Qal 'break/shatter' (كسر); Niphal 'be broken' (انكسر Form VII); Piel intensive 'shatter/smash' (كسّر Form II); Niphal ptc 'broken state' (esp. broken-hearted, منكسر); Hifil 'cause to break forth' (birth); Hofal 'be crushed'; metaphorical 'quench thirst'; extended 'crush/oppress' (ليسحق — different Arabic root). Swahili -vunja/-vunjwa/-vunjika tracked binyanim precisely."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3467","labelChirho":"ישׁע save","beforeChirho":111,"afterChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Root ישׁע: Hifil active 'save/deliver/rescue' (خلّص, 81 clusters — vast majority purely inflectional splits); Hifil ptc substantive 'savior/deliverer' (مخلّص); Niphal passive 'be saved' (خُلّص/يخلص, Korean 구원받다); abstract noun 'salvation' (نجدة). Very uniform root — 111→4 reflects high inflectional over-splitting."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7592","labelChirho":"שׁאל ask","beforeChirho":111,"afterChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Root שׁאל: Qal 'ask/inquire' (سأل) vs 'request/petition/demand' (طلب — different Arabic root confirms genuine split); Qal passive ptc 'borrow' (مستعار); Hifil causative 'lend' (أعار); Niphal reflexive 'ask leave' (استأذن); Piel intensive 'beg' (구걸하다). Arabic سأل vs طلب as primary discriminator within Qal."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7843","labelChirho":"שׁחת destroy/corrupt","beforeChirho":109,"afterChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Root שׁחת: primary split is physical destruction (أهلك) vs moral corruption (أفسد) — confirmed by Arabic and Korean 멸하다 vs 타락하다. Within each: Hifil vs Piel by binyan; Niphal passive 'be corrupted' (فسد intransitive); Hofal 'be blemished' (ritual/sacrificial); distinct 'spoil/waste' (أضاع). Destruction vs corruption is the key semantic axis."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G4160","labelChirho":"ποιέω do/make","beforeChirho":108,"afterChirho":11,"notesChirho":"Greek ποιέω: 'do/act/perform' (فعل) vs 'make/construct' (صنع) vs 'cause/render' (جعل) — 3-way Arabic split mirrors Hebrew עשׂה. Additional: 'produce/bear fruit' (أثمر); 'appoint' (أقام); 'keep/celebrate' (feast context, Korean 지키다); 'spend time' (قضى); 'work/labor' (عمل); 'commit/perpetrate' (저지르다); 'compose/record' (기록하다); 'consider/reckon' (여기다). BDAG-aligned."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6030b","labelChirho":"ענה answer","beforeChirho":107,"afterChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Root ענה II: dominant 'answer/respond' (أجاب, 89 clusters — inflectional over-splitting); 'testify/bear witness' (شهد — completely different Arabic root); Niphal passive 'be answered'; specialized 'sing in response' (تغنّي, antiphonal singing); homonym ענה III 'be brought low/silenced' (تُخفت — unrelated root). Arabic أجاب vs شهد vs تغنّي as key discriminators."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7901","labelChirho":"שׁכב lie down","beforeChirho":107,"afterChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Root שׁכב: Qal core 'lie down/rest/sleep' (اضطجع, 88 clusters — massive inflectional over-splitting); Qal 'lie with sexually' (ضاجع, Korean 동침했다); Hifil causative 'cause to lie down' (أضجع); rare Hifil 'tip over/pour out' (يُسكب — completely different Arabic root, Job 38:37); Hofal passive 'be laid down'; Niphal/Pual 'be sexually violated'. Arabic اضطجع vs ضاجع vs يُسكب as key discriminators."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1697","labelChirho":"דָּבָר word/thing","beforeChirho":106,"afterChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Nominal דָּבָר: 'word/speech/utterance' (كلمة/كلام, 67 clusters) vs 'matter/affair' (أمر) vs 'thing/something' (شيء) vs 'deeds/chronicles' (أمور/أخبار) vs 'happenings/events' vs 'manner/likeness' (كالأمر) vs 'cause/reason' (سبب). Arabic كلمة vs أمر vs شيء vs سبب as primary 4-way discriminator."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1323","labelChirho":"בַּת daughter","beforeChirho":105,"afterChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Nominal בַּת: 'daughter/female offspring' (بنت, 98 clusters — noun with almost no genuine polysemy, purely inflectional splits) vs 'dependent village/satellite town' (7 clusters — Arabic retains بنات but Korean 마을들, Swahili vijiji, Spanish aldeas all switch to village vocabulary). Most aggressive merge in the pipeline: 105→2."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6942","labelChirho":"קדשׁ be holy","beforeChirho":105,"afterChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Root קדשׁ: clean binyan-based split — Qal stative 'be holy' (قدُس); Niphal 'be shown/proved holy' (divine self-vindication); Piel factitive 'sanctify/consecrate' (قدّس Form II, 41 clusters); Pual passive 'be consecrated' (مقدّس); Hifil causative 'dedicate/set apart'; Hitpael reflexive 'sanctify oneself' (تقدّس Form V); nominal 'holy things'. Arabic Forms matching Hebrew binyanim precisely."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6944","labelChirho":"קֹדֶשׁ holiness","beforeChirho":105,"afterChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Nominal קֹדֶשׁ: 'holiness (abstract)' (قُدس); 'sanctuary/holy place' (مقدِس, Swahili patakatifu locative); 'holiness with possessive pronouns'; 'holy thing (singular)' (مقدّس, Swahili kitakatifu ki-class); 'holy things (plural)' (أقداس, Swahili vitakatifu vi-class, 36 clusters); 'holy of holies' (superlative construct). Swahili noun class system (u-/pa-/ki-/vi-) mapped precisely to sense distinctions."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3709","labelChirho":"כַּף palm/sole","beforeChirho":102,"afterChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Nominal כַּף: 'palm of hand' (كفّ, 73 clusters); 'sole of foot' (باطن/أسفل — different Arabic); 'paw' (Hindi पंजा); 'hip socket' (حقّ — different Arabic); 'ladle/dish/spoon' (كؤوس/صحون — cultic vessels); 'handle of bolt' (كفّوت); 'palm branch/frond' (سعف — different Arabic). Korean 손바닥 vs 발바닥 cleanly separated hand from foot."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3680","labelChirho":"כסה cover","beforeChirho":101,"afterChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Root כסה: Piel 'cover physically' (غطّى) vs 'conceal/hide' (ستر/كتم/أخفى — 3 different Arabic roots!) vs 'clothe/dress' (كسا — yet another Arabic root) vs 'overwhelm/engulf' (غشي/غمر); Pual/Niphal passive 'be covered'; Hitpael reflexive 'cover oneself'. Arabic provided 5+ distinct roots for what Hebrew expressed with one — strongest polysemy evidence yet."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0954","labelChirho":"בושׁ be ashamed","beforeChirho":50,"afterChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Root בושׁ: Qal stative 'be ashamed' (خزي/خجل); Hifil causative 'put to shame' (مُخزٍ, Swahili -aibisha with -isha causative); Piel 'delay/tarry' (أبطأ — completely different domain, well-known secondary development); rare archaic 'dry up/wither' (يجفّ — all 6 languages agree on desiccation not shame)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1058","labelChirho":"בכה weep","beforeChirho":50,"afterChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Root בכה: Qal 'weep/cry' (بكى, 47 clusters — uniform Arabic across all inflections); Piel 'bewail/lament for' (transitive, Swahili -mlia vs -lia); noun בֶּכִי 'weeping/lamentation'. Minimal polysemy — 50→3 reflects purely inflectional over-splitting."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2077","labelChirho":"זֶבַח sacrifice","beforeChirho":50,"afterChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Nominal זֶבַח: 'sacrifice/animal offering' (ذبيحة/ذبائح, 48 clusters — purely grammatical splits); 'sacrificial feast/communal meal' (Swahili karamu instead of dhabihu); 'altar' (مذابحهم — place-noun pattern, likely data-mapping issue with H4196 מזבח). Noun with near-zero polysemy."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3289","labelChirho":"יעץ counsel","beforeChirho":50,"afterChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Root יעץ: Qal 'counsel/advise' (أشار) vs 'purpose/plan/determine' (قصد/قضى — different Arabic roots); Qal ptc substantive 'counselor' (مشير, 16 clusters); Niphal/Hitpael reciprocal 'consult together' (تشاور/استشار); Qal passive 'that which is purposed' (المقصود). Arabic أشار vs قصد vs تشاور as key discriminators."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4758","labelChirho":"מַרְאֶה appearance/vision","beforeChirho":50,"afterChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Nominal מַרְאֶה: 'appearance/outward form' (منظر, 35 clusters); 'vision/prophetic sight' (الرؤيا, Korean 환상); 'sight/act of seeing' (رؤية/عيان). Arabic منظر vs الرؤيا vs رؤية as clean 3-way discriminator. Minimal genuine polysemy — mostly inflectional over-splitting."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5027","labelChirho":"נבט look","beforeChirho":50,"afterChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Root נבט: primarily Hifil 'look/gaze' (نظر, 36 clusters — Arabic uniform); directional 'look down from above' (Korean 내려다보다); evaluative 'regard/observe attentively' (Korean 눈여겨보다); compassionate 'look upon with concern' (Korean 돌아보다); single Piel occurrence. Korean agglutinative forms provided the best discriminators — Arabic was too uniform."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5656","labelChirho":"עֲבֹדָה service/work","beforeChirho":50,"afterChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Nominal עֲבֹדָה: 'cultic service of tabernacle' (خدمة in sacred context); 'general service/ministry' (خدمة in secular context); 'work/labor' (عمل — different Arabic root); 'bondage/servitude' (عبودية — yet another Arabic root). Arabic خدمة vs عمل vs عبودية as clean 3-way discriminator."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5782","labelChirho":"עור awake/rouse","beforeChirho":50,"afterChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Root עור: clean binyan-based split — Qal 'awake' (استيقظ); Niphal 'be roused' (passive); Piel 'rouse/stir up' (أيقظ/يثير); distinct Piel 'wield/brandish' (شهر/هزّ — completely different Arabic, Korean 휘두르다); Hifil causative 'awaken/stir up' (21 clusters); Hitpael reflexive 'rouse oneself' (يتحرّك). The 'brandish' sub-sense confirmed by total Arabic/Korean vocabulary switch."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H8248","labelChirho":"שׁקה give drink","beforeChirho":50,"afterChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Root שׁקה: Hifil 'give drink to beings' (سقى) vs 'water/irrigate land' (Spanish regar vs dar de beber, Hindi सींचना vs पिलाना); Pual passive 'be watered'; Niphal textual variant 'sink down' (تغور — completely different domain). Spanish and Hindi provided clearest animate-vs-inanimate split."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G1544","labelChirho":"ἐκβάλλω cast out","beforeChirho":49,"afterChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Greek ἐκβάλλω: 'cast out/expel' (أخرج/طرد, 29 clusters — exorcism + expulsion); 'send out/dispatch' (Korean 보내다); 'bring forth/produce' (Korean 꺼내다); 'take out/extract' (Korean 빼다 — speck from eye); 'throw out/reject violently' (ألقوه/اطرحوه); passive 'be cast out'. Korean agglutinative forms provided the best discriminators."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1234","labelChirho":"בקע cleave/split","beforeChirho":49,"afterChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Root בקע: 5 distinct Arabic roots for one Hebrew root — شقّ (split), اقتحم (breach/storm), مزّق (tear apart), انفجر (gush/burst forth), فقس (hatch eggs). Plus passive/reflexive forms. Strongest Arabic polysemy evidence in the pipeline — 5 unrelated Arabic roots mapping to sub-senses of a single Hebrew root."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5892b","labelChirho":"עִיר city","beforeChirho":49,"afterChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Nominal עִיר: completely monosemous — 49→1. Arabic مدينة, Korean 성읍, Swahili mji, Hindi नगर all uniform across all 49 clusters. Purely inflectional over-splitting (articles, prepositions, pronominal suffixes, number). Most aggressive merge in entire pipeline."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7451a","labelChirho":"רַע evil/bad","beforeChirho":49,"afterChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Adjective רַע: 'morally evil/wicked' (شرّير); 'evil/harm/calamity' (شرّ, abstract substantive); 'bad/poor quality' (رديء — different Arabic root); 'ugly/ill-favored' (قبيح — another Arabic root); 'sad/displeased' (حزين); 'severe/fierce/dangerous' (Korean 심한/사나운). Arabic شرّير vs رديء vs قبيح vs حزين as 4-way discriminator."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G0991","labelChirho":"βλέπω see/look","beforeChirho":48,"afterChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Greek βλέπω: 'see/perceive visually' (21 clusters); 'look at/direct gaze' (intentional); imperative 'look!/see!' (directive); 'beware/watch out' (احذروا/조심하라 — cautionary, well-attested BDAG sense); middle/passive 'be visible'; future/prophetic 'will see'; imperfect 'were gazing intently'. Arabic احذروا cleanly separated the 'beware' sense."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0068","labelChirho":"אֶבֶן stone","beforeChirho":48,"afterChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Nominal אֶבֶן: 'stone/rock' (43 clusters — minimal polysemy); metonymic 'weight/balance-stone' (Korean 저울추, Hindi बाट); 'precious stone/gem' (Korean 보석, Swahili vito); divine epithet 'Stone of Israel' (Hindi चट्टान, Arabic صخرة — shift from 'stone' to 'rock/cliff'). Predominantly monosemous noun."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0127","labelChirho":"אֲדָמָה ground/soil","beforeChirho":48,"afterChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Nominal אֲדָמָה: 'ground/land/territory' (38 clusters); 'soil/dust (substance)' (Arabic تراب, Swahili udongo); 'ground surface (underfoot)'; 'farmland/arable ground' (Arabic حقولكم, Korean 토지); 'lands (plural territories)'. Arabic أرض vs تراب vs حقول as key discriminators."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1692","labelChirho":"דבק cling/cleave","beforeChirho":48,"afterChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Root דבק: Qal 'cling physically' vs 'cling in devotion' (relational — Spanish apegarse/unirse vs pegarse); Pual/Hofal passive 'stick together'; Hifil causative 'make stick/cause to cling'; Hifil 'pursue closely/overtake' (military — completely different domain from adhesion). Physical vs relational adhesion split confirmed by Spanish."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2199","labelChirho":"זעק cry out","beforeChirho":48,"afterChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Root זעק: Qal 'cry out/call for help' (37 clusters — dominant); Niphal 'be called together/assemble' (wakaitwa/walikusanyika); Hifil 'summon/call together' (convocó); Hifil 'proclaim/cry aloud' (hizo proclamar). Hifil split into assembly-summoning vs proclamation."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2308","labelChirho":"חדל cease/stop","beforeChirho":48,"afterChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Root חדל: 'cease/come to an end' (intransitive cessation); 'desist/refrain from' (volitional holding back); 'leave alone/let be' (imperative/jussive — stop bothering); 'fail/be lacking' (cessation of existence). Semantic splits based on agency and transitivity rather than binyan."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3220","labelChirho":"יָם sea","beforeChirho":48,"afterChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Nominal יָם: 'sea/body of water' (بحر, 21 clusters); 'Sea of [geographic name]' (construct-state proper names, 13 clusters); 'west/westward' (directional metonymy from Mediterranean lying west, 14 clusters — יָמָּה with directional he). Geographic metonymy is a genuine lexicographic sense."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3782","labelChirho":"כשׁל stumble","beforeChirho":48,"afterChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Root כשׁל: Qal 'stumble/trip' vs 'become weak/fail' (semantic shift from motion to debility); Niphal 'stumble and fall' (resultative); Hifil causative 'cause to stumble'; Hofal passive 'be made to stumble'; Piel ketiv 'bereave/cause childlessness' (distinct textual variant). Clean binyan-based split with Qal sub-sense separation."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3899","labelChirho":"לֶחֶם bread/food","beforeChirho":48,"afterChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Nominal לֶחֶם: 'bread/loaf' (خبز/pan/mkate, 35 clusters); 'food/sustenance' (طعام/comida/chakula — generic food words, 12 clusters); 'produce/yield of land' (بثمرها/na matunda — agricultural harvest, 1 cluster). Translation vocabulary switch bread→food is the key discriminator."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4196","labelChirho":"מִזְבֵּחַ altar","beforeChirho":48,"afterChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Nominal מִזְבֵּחַ: completely monosemous — 48→1. All splits purely grammatical (articles, prepositions, construct state, pronominal suffixes, number). Arabic مذبح, Korean 제단, Swahili madhabahu uniform throughout. Third monosemous noun in pipeline (after עיר 49→1 and similar)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5341","glossChirho":"נצר","fromChirho":48,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"guard/keep/watch + preserve/maintain + besiege (Qal military) + Nifal passive + hidden/secret things (subst.)"}
{"lemmaChirho":"G0622","glossChirho":"ἀπόλλυμι","fromChirho":47,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"destroy/kill (active transitive) + perish/die (middle intransitive) + lose/misplace + be lost/ruined (passive)"}
{"lemmaChirho":"G1510","glossChirho":"εἰμί","fromChirho":47,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"copula (X is Y) + exist/be present + happen/occur + belong to + be in a state/condition + it is possible (impersonal)"}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0631","glossChirho":"אסר","fromChirho":47,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"bind/tie physically (Qal) + bind by vow (Num 30) + harness animals + prisoner (subst. ptcpl) + be bound (Nif/Pual) + begin to stir (disputed, Judg 13:25)"}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3885a","glossChirho":"לין","fromChirho":47,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"lodge overnight (persons, primary) + remain overnight (things — fat, wages, anger) + dwell/abide (extended habitation). Arabic بات vs سكن distinguishes overnight vs permanent."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5157","glossChirho":"נחל","fromChirho":47,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Qal inherit + Piel distribute/apportion + Hifil cause to inherit + Hitpael take possession + Hofal be allotted. Arabic Form I وَرِثَ vs Form II قَسَّمَ vs Form IV أَوْرَثَ cleanly separates binyanim."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5439","glossChirho":"סביב","fromChirho":47,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"around/all around (spatial) + surroundings/those around (substantive) + circuit/course (cyclical, 1 occ). Arabic حَوْلَ vs بَيْنَ vs دَوَرَان distinguishes."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7521","glossChirho":"רצה","fromChirho":47,"toChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Qal be pleased + Qal accept favorably + enjoy/pay off sabbaths + Qal pass ptcp favored + Nifal be accepted + Piel seek favor + Hitpael make oneself acceptable. Korean 기뻐하다 vs 받다 split."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H8432","glossChirho":"תוך","fromChirho":47,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"midst/middle (spatial) + among/in community of + interior/inside + midpoint (absolute). Arabic وسط vs بين vs داخل vs الوسط. Korean 가운데 vs 중 vs 속 vs 중간."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G1438","glossChirho":"ἑαυτοῦ","fromChirho":46,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"reflexive pronoun (direct/indirect) + possessive reflexive (of oneself) + reciprocal (one another) + comparative (than oneself). Over-splitting was purely inflectional."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G2147","glossChirho":"εὑρίσκω","fromChirho":46,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"find/discover (active) + be found/discovered (passive) + obtain/secure (1 occ, Heb 9:12). Arabic وَجَدَ vs وُجِدَ split. BDAG sense 'obtain' confirmed by Korean 얻다."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G4982","glossChirho":"σῴζω","fromChirho":46,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"rescue from danger + save spiritually + heal/restore to health + make whole through faith (bridging). Arabic نَجِّ vs خَلَّصَ vs شَفَى splits cleanly."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1004b","glossChirho":"בַּיִת","fromChirho":46,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"house/dwelling (physical, 39 clusters — nearly all inflectional) + household/dynasty + inside/interior (Arabic داخل, Korean 안) + temple (1 occ). 39→1 for physical house shows extreme inflectional over-splitting."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1157","glossChirho":"בְּעַד","fromChirho":46,"toChirho":7,"notesChirho":"on behalf of + through/via + behind/after + around/encircling + on account of + in exchange for (Arabic بَدَلَ) + temporal. Highly polysemous preposition."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2505a","glossChirho":"חלק","fromChirho":46,"toChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Qal divide + Piel distribute + Piel scatter (1 occ, diaspora) + Hifil allot + Nifal be divided + Pual be distributed + Hitpael divide among selves. Arabic Forms I/II/VII/VIII map precisely to Hebrew binyanim."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2781","glossChirho":"חֶרְפָּה","fromChirho":46,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"disgrace/shame (state, 41 clusters) + taunts/verbal reproach (Arabic تَعْيِير) + shameful defect/blemish (Arabic عَيْب). 41→1 for core sense shows extreme inflectional over-splitting."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4687","glossChirho":"מִצְוָה","fromChirho":46,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"commandment/divine precept (Arabic وَصِيَّة, 41 clusters) + command/directive (Arabic أَمْر, 5 clusters). Nearly monosemous noun."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7393","glossChirho":"רֶכֶב","fromChirho":46,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"chariot/chariotry (44 clusters) + upper millstone (2 clusters — Arabic رَحًى, Korean 맷돌). Metaphorical transfer from 'riding' to the stone that rides on the lower."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7673a","glossChirho":"שׁבת","fromChirho":46,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Qal cease + Qal rest/observe Sabbath (Arabic اِسْتَرَاحَ vs بَطَلَ) + Nifal be brought to end + Hifil cause to cease + Hifil remove/abolish + Hifil still/silence."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H8034","glossChirho":"שֵׁם","fromChirho":46,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"name/designation (42 clusters, Arabic اسم) + fame/reputation/renown (4 clusters, Swahili sifa, Spanish renombre). Nearly monosemous."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H8628","glossChirho":"תקע","fromChirho":46,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"blow trumpet (Qal) + be blown (Nifal) + thrust/drive in (peg, Arabic غَرَزَ) + clap hands (Arabic صَفَّقوا) + pledge hands as surety (Arabic ضامِنينَ). Root means 'strike/thrust' with divergent applications."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G3195","glossChirho":"μέλλω","fromChirho":45,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"be about to (imminent) + coming/future (adjectival) + intend/be ready + delay/hesitate (Acts 22:16). All languages independently confirm delay sense with different vocabulary."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0517","glossChirho":"אֵם","fromChirho":45,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"mother/female parent (43 clusters, Arabic أُمّ) + mother animal/dam (2 clusters — Korean 어미 vs 어머니 is decisive split signal)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1241","glossChirho":"בָּקָר","fromChirho":45,"toChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Completely monosemous: cattle/herd. All 45 clusters purely inflectional. Arabic بَقَر, Korean 소, Swahili ng'ombe uniformly throughout."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1369","glossChirho":"גְּבוּרָה","fromChirho":45,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"might/power + mighty deeds (plural concrete) + valor/martial prowess (Arabic بَأْس) + warriors (metonymy, Arabic أَبطَال) + victory (Arabic غَلَبَة). Four distinct Arabic roots confirm boundaries."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1755","glossChirho":"דור","fromChirho":45,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"generation/contemporaries + perpetuity (מִדּוֹר לְדוֹר formula, Korean 대대로) + generations/posterity (plural cultic) + dwelling/habitation (1 occ, Isa 38:12, Arabic دَوْر)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3335","glossChirho":"יצר","fromChirho":45,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"potter/artisan (Arabic فَخَّار) + form/fashion/create (Qal) + purpose/plan (Korean 계획하다) + Nifal be formed + Pual be formed + Hofal be formed. Korean uniquely distinguishes planning sense."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6588","glossChirho":"פֶּשַׁע","fromChirho":45,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"transgression/offense (40 clusters, Arabic مَعْصِيَة) + rebellion/revolt (5 clusters, Arabic عِصْيَان). Nearly monosemous noun."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G5083","glossChirho":"τηρέω","fromChirho":44,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"observe/obey commands + guard/protect + hold in custody (1 occ) + reserve/preserve for future. Hindi मानना vs सुरक्षित रखना cleanly splits obey from guard."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1104","glossChirho":"בלע","fromChirho":44,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"swallow (Qal) + devour (Piel intensive) + destroy (Piel metaphorical, Arabic يُزِيلُ) + confuse/confound (Piel) + be swallowed (Nif/Pual) + be engulfed (Hitpael). Three Piel sub-senses confirmed cross-linguistically."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1481a","glossChirho":"גור","fromChirho":44,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Qal sojourn as alien (Arabic تَغَرَّبَ) + sojourner subst. ptcp + dwell/lodge (Arabic يَسْكُنُ) + guest/lodger (Arabic نَزِيلَة). Arabic root shift غ-ر-ب vs س-ك-ن vs ن-ز-ل is decisive."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2220","glossChirho":"זְרוֹעַ","fromChirho":44,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"arm/physical limb (38 clusters) + might/power/forces (metonymic, Swahili nguvu vs mkono) + shoulder/foreleg of animal (Korean 어깨 vs 팔). Nearly monosemous."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3581b","glossChirho":"כֹּחַ","fromChirho":44,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"strength/force/might (35 clusters) + power/ability/capability (Arabic القُدْرَة, Korean 능력) + wealth/resources (1 occ, Korean 재물, Swahili utajiri)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3898a","glossChirho":"לחם","fromChirho":44,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Nifal fight/wage war (40 clusters, Arabic حارَبَ) + Qal fighter/combatant (4 clusters, ptcp). Nearly monosemous verb. Arabic cognate لَحَمَ shifted to 'solder' — interesting semantic drift."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3956","glossChirho":"לָשׁוֹן","fromChirho":44,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"tongue/organ (Arabic لِسَان) + language (Arabic لُغَة, Korean 언어) + bay/tongue of sea (Hindi खाड़ी) + bar/wedge (Korean 혀모양). Swahili ulimi/lugha/ghuba/kipande maps 1:1 to 4 senses."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6666","glossChirho":"צְדָקָה","fromChirho":44,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"righteousness/moral uprightness (Arabic بِرّ, 34 clusters) + righteous acts (plural צִדְקוֹת) + right/legal entitlement (1 occ, Arabic حَقّ, Korean 권리)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6862c","glossChirho":"צַר","fromChirho":44,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"adversary/enemy (39 clusters, Arabic عَدُوّ) + distress/trouble (5 clusters, Arabic ضِيق cognate). Clean person vs abstract split."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G0630","glossChirho":"ἀπολύω","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"release/set free (Arabic أَطْلَقَ Form IV) + divorce (Arabic طَلَّقَ Form II — same root different form!) + dismiss/send away (Arabic صَرَفَ) + forgive (Arabic غ-ف-ر) + depart (Arabic انصَرَفَ Form VII)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G1410","glossChirho":"δύναμαι","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"be able/can (finite, 35 clusters) + the powerful/mighty one (substantival ptcp, 8 clusters). Korean 할 수 있다 vs 능한 distinguishes."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G3306","glossChirho":"μένω","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"stay/lodge (Arabic مَكَثَ) + abide/persist (Arabic ثَبَتَ, Johannine) + last/endure (Arabic بَاقٍ) + wait for/await (Arabic انْتَظَرَ). Four distinct Arabic verbs confirm boundaries."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0120","glossChirho":"אָדָם","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"man/human being (Arabic إِنْسَان) + mankind/humanity (Arabic بَشَر/نَاس) + Adam proper name (Arabic آدَم). Arabic root shift is decisive."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1121a","glossChirho":"בֵּן","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"son/descendant (35 clusters, Arabic ابن) + children/collective (Korean 자녀들 vs 아들) + young of animals (Arabic عِجْل, Korean 새끼). Animal offspring sense confirmed by total lexical shift in all languages."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1419a","glossChirho":"גָּדוֹל","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"great/large (adj) + elder/older (Arabic كَبِير) + great things (subst. deeds) + loud (Arabic عالٍ) + nobles/great ones (Arabic عُظَماء) + becoming great (verbal inf abs)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2205","glossChirho":"זָקֵן","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"elder/community leader (Korean 장로, 30 clusters) + old/aged person (Korean 늙은/노인) + old woman (Arabic عَجَائِز, different root). Korean 장로 vs 노인 is decisive."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2232","glossChirho":"זרע","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"sow seed (Qal) + be sown (Nif/Pual passive) + yield seed (Hifil, Gen 1) + conceive/become pregnant (Arabic حَبِلَ, Korean 잉태하다) + scatter/disperse (Korean 흩트리다). Conception sense confirmed by non-agricultural vocab in all languages."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2416a","glossChirho":"חַי","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"alive/living (adj) + living creature/animal (Arabic حَيَوَان) + oath formula 'as X lives' (19 clusters) + raw/fresh (Arabic نَيِّئ) + will live (verbal, 1 occ)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2719","glossChirho":"חֶרֶב","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"sword/weapon (41 clusters, Arabic سَيْف) + knife/cutting blade (Arabic سَكاكين, Josh 5) + ruin/desolation (1 occ, homophonous root חרב II). Nearly monosemous."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3707","glossChirho":"כעס","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"be angry/vexed (Qal intrans) + provoke to anger (Hifil/Piel causative, 36 clusters) + vex/taunt interpersonally (Swahili -chokoza vs -kasirisha). Piel crosses both causative and interpersonal."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3722a","glossChirho":"כפר","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":7,"notesChirho":"ritual atonement (Piel, 27 clusters) + appease/pacify + cover/conceal sin + forgive directly + avert harm + be atoned (Pual/Hitpael) + be annulled (Korean 폐하다). Rich polysemy confirmed by Korean 속죄/달래/덮/용서/막/폐."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4735","glossChirho":"מִקְנֶה","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"livestock/cattle (37 clusters, Arabic مَاشِيَة) + acquisition/purchase/possession (Arabic شِرَاء, Korean 소유). Root q-n-h 'acquire' retains abstract sense in 6 occurrences."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5030","glossChirho":"נָבִיא prophet","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Completely monosemous — all 43 senses unified into one. Arabic nabiyy/anbiya, Korean seonjiJa, Swahili nabii all render uniformly with zero translation-signature divergence. No evidence for splitting false vs true prophets or individual vs collective."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5080","glossChirho":"נדח drive away","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":9,"notesChirho":"Rich binyan-driven polysemy: Hifil splits into banish vs lead-astray vs thrust-down; Nifal into be-banished vs be-enticed vs straying vs slip; Hofal/Pual as driven/hunted; rare Qal swing/brandish. Arabic طرد vs أضل vs يدفع cleanly separate the Hifil senses. Korean 쫓아내다 vs 빗나게 하다 vs 미끄러지다 confirm."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5797","glossChirho":"עֹז strength","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Core abstract strength (40 senses) plus 3 minority senses: fortress/stronghold (Arabic حصن, Swahili ngome), fierceness/brazenness (Arabic قساوة, Swahili ugumu), and fortified/impregnable (Arabic حصين). The fortress sense is a concrete metaphorical extension."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6381","glossChirho":"פלא be wonderful","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Binyan-driven splits: Nifal ptc 'wonders' (19 senses) vs Nifal stative 'be marvelous' vs 'be too difficult' (Arabic yaʿsuru shifts from wonder to impossibility); Hifil 'do wondrously' vs 'make extraordinary vow' (cultic technical); Piel 'fulfill special vow'; Hitpael 'show oneself marvelous'. Korean 기이한 일 vs 너무 어렵다 cleanly separates wonder from difficulty."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7323","glossChirho":"רוץ run","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Qal splits into generic running vs participle-as-noun (runner) vs two institutional roles: royal guard (1-2 Kings harratsim as bodyguard corps — Korean 친위대원/경비병, Swahili walinzi) and courier/messenger (2 Chr 30, Esther postal runners — Korean 배달자/급사, Swahili wajumbe). Plus Piel intensive dart and Hifil causative chase/hurry."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H8334","glossChirho":"שׁרת minister, serve","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Clean verbal vs substantival participle split. Piel-only verb — the participle mesharet is extensively substantivized as 'minister/servant/attendant' (royal, priestly, personal) while the finite forms remain purely verbal 'to serve/minister'. Standard BDB/HALOT distinction."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H8582","glossChirho":"תעה go astray","fromChirho":43,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Binyan-driven: Qal physical wandering vs Qal moral erring vs Qal/Nifal staggering (drunkenness); Hifil causative 'lead astray' (largest group at 20); Nifal passive 'be deceived'. Arabic تاه (wander) vs ضل (err) vs ترنح (stagger) clearly separate the Qal senses."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G3708","glossChirho":"ὁράω see","fromChirho":42,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Core visual perception (32 clusters) plus exclamatory behold/look (interjection POS tag confirms — Spanish 'he aquí', Hindi देखो), passive 'appear/become visible' (divine manifestation), mental 'perceive/understand' (Spanish percibir), and deliberate 'look at/gaze upon'. The behold sense is by far the most distinctive split."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G4043","glossChirho":"περιπατέω walk","fromChirho":42,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Literal walk vs figurative conduct/live (Pauline epistolary dominant) vs roam/walk-about. Arabic مشى (walk physically) vs سلك (conduct/follow a course) is the clearest discriminator. Korean 걷다 (walk) vs 행하다 (act/practice) confirms. The roam sense uses unique Arabic يجول (go about)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0269","glossChirho":"אָחוֹת sister","fromChirho":42,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Nearly monosemous — 40/42 clusters are literal female sibling across all inflections. The 2-cluster minority sense is the Hebrew idiom אשה אל-אחותה ('one to another') applied to non-personal objects (curtain panels, cherub wings). Hindi दूसरे/दूसरी ('another') and Swahili nyingine ('other') clearly distinguish from kinship."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3001","glossChirho":"יבשׁ be dry","fromChirho":42,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Intransitive Qal/Nifal 'dry up/wither' (28 senses) vs causative Hifil/Piel 'cause to dry up' (9) vs semantically distinct Hifil 'put to shame' (5). The shame sense is a genuine homonym — Arabic خزي (be ashamed) vs جف (be dry), Korean 부끄러워 vs 마르다 clearly separate."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4194","glossChirho":"מָוֶת death","fromChirho":42,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six senses: event of dying (21), death as penalty/fate (8), personified Death as cosmic power (4, Song 8:6, Isa 28:15), state/realm of the dead (4), comparative 'like death' (4), and plural 'kinds of deaths' (1). Arabic الموت vs حكم الموت vs الموت personified shows some distinction but Korean 죽음 is uniform across most."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4264","glossChirho":"מַחֲנֶה camp","fromChirho":42,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Mostly monosemous — 38/42 clusters are physical camp/encampment. Metonymic 'army/host' (3 clusters) confirmed by Arabic جيش, Hindi सेना, Swahili jeshi instead of camp terms. Dual form Mahanaim (1 cluster) as place name."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6098","glossChirho":"עֵצָה counsel","fromChirho":42,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Core 'advice/counsel' (26) vs 'plan/purpose/design' (8, especially divine — Arabic القصد, Swahili kusudi) vs 'schemes/plots' (7, negative plural — Arabic المكائد) vs 'counselor' (1, substantivized participle). The plan vs advice distinction is the key split."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6555","glossChirho":"פרץ break through","fromChirho":42,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Rich polysemy: break-through/burst-forth (13) vs break-down/demolish (6) vs passive broken/breached (6 Pual/Qal passive) vs spread-out/multiply (13) vs press/urge socially (3) vs Hitpael break-away/scatter (1). The spread/increase sense is distinctive — Arabic انتشر (spread) vs اقتحم (break through)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6635a","glossChirho":"צָבָא host, army","fromChirho":42,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Military force/army (36 clusters) vs warfare/campaign activity (2, Arabic للحرب, Swahili vita) vs hard service/compulsory duty (4, Job 7:1 and Isa 40:2 — Arabic خدمة, Korean 복무). The service sense is well-attested in BDB as a distinct meaning."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7364","glossChirho":"רחץ wash","fromChirho":42,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Intransitive 'bathe body' (17, Arabic يستحم, Swahili -oga) vs transitive 'wash object/body-part' (22, Arabic غسل, Swahili -osha/-nawa) vs Pual passive 'be washed' (2) vs Hitpael reflexive 'wash oneself' (1). The bathe vs wash split is the key distinction — Arabic uses entirely different verbs."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7379","glossChirho":"רִיב strife, case","fromChirho":42,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three-way split: interpersonal strife/quarrel (14, Hindi झगड़ा, Swahili ugomvi) vs formal legal case/lawsuit (20, Korean 송사, Swahili kesi) vs broader dispute/controversy (8, Hindi विवाद, Swahili mabishano). The legal vs non-legal distinction is the primary axis."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7442b","glossChirho":"רנן shout for joy","fromChirho":42,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Binyan-driven: Qal joyful singing (11) vs Qal urgent crying out (2) vs Piel intensive exultation (22, largest) vs Hifil causative (5) vs Pual passive (1) vs Hitpael reflexive-intensive (1). The Qal cry-out vs joy distinction is notable — Spanish clama vs canta."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7503","glossChirho":"רפה be slack","fromChirho":42,"toChirho":9,"notesChirho":"Highly polysemous across stems: Qal be-slack/grow-weak (10) vs Nifal/Hitpael be-lazy (4) vs Piel weaken-others (2) vs Piel let-drop-physically (2) vs Hifil let-go/release (10) vs Hifil abandon/forsake (5) vs Hifil cease/stop (5) vs Qal sink/decline (3) vs Qal relent (1). Nine senses from 42 is high but justified by the semantic range across 4 binyanim."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G0025","glossChirho":"ἀγαπάω love","fromChirho":41,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Active love (34 clusters) vs perfect passive participle 'beloved' as substantive (5) vs non-participial passive 'be loved' (2). The beloved sense is a genuine lexicographic distinction — used as direct address/title rather than verbal action."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G0071","glossChirho":"ἄγω bring, lead","fromChirho":41,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Physical bring/lead (32) vs figurative spiritual guidance (5, Rom 8:14 Spirit-led) vs intransitive 'let us go' (2) vs 'celebrate events' (1) vs 'spend time' (1, Arabic يمضي). The figurative spiritual guidance is the most interesting split."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0214","glossChirho":"אוֹצָר treasure","fromChirho":41,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Treasure/valuables (12) vs treasury/treasure-house (24) vs storehouse/granary (5). Arabic كنوز vs خزائن vs مخازن cleanly separates all three. Korean 보물 vs 창고 vs 곳간 confirms."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1167","glossChirho":"בַּעַל owner, lord","fromChirho":41,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Husband (7) vs property owner/master (20) vs civic lord/ruler (10) vs 'one characterized by X' idiom (2) vs ally/confederate (1) vs chief/officer (1). Arabic زوج vs صاحب vs أصحاب/سادة provides the clearest separation."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1471a","glossChirho":"גּוֹי nation","fromChirho":41,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Nearly monosemous — singular nation (13) vs plural nations (27) vs proper name Goyim (1, Gen 14 Tidal). The singular/plural split is marginal but the translation signatures do differ slightly. The Goyim proper name is clearly distinct."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2491a","glossChirho":"חָלָל slain","fromChirho":41,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Slain/fatally pierced (30) vs wounded/injured (8, Arabic جريح, Spanish herido) vs corpse/carcass (3, Korean 시체, Swahili maiti). The wounded vs slain distinction is well-attested — different Arabic roots confirm."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3519","glossChirho":"כָּבוֹד glory","fromChirho":41,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Divine glory/splendor (31) vs honor/respect/dignity (6, Arabic كرامة vs مجد) vs wealth/abundance (2) vs nobles/honored ones (1, metonymic) vs soul/inner self (1, psalmic parallel to 'heart'). The glory vs honor split is the key distinction."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3605","glossChirho":"כֹּל all","fromChirho":41,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Attributive all/every/whole (30) vs substantival everything/totality (3) vs pronominal all-of-them (2) vs indefinite any/anything (4) vs distributive each/every (2). Function word with genuine syntactic polysemy rather than semantic."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4397","glossChirho":"מַלְאָךְ messenger","fromChirho":41,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Angel/divine messenger (17, Arabic ملاك, Korean 천사, Swahili malaika) vs human messenger/envoy (24, Arabic رسول, Korean 사자, Swahili mjumbe). The translation signatures are perfectly bimodal — different lexemes in every language."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4592","glossChirho":"מְעַט little, few","fromChirho":41,"toChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Seven senses: small quantity (10), few in number (8), almost/nearly כמעט (4), soon/momentarily (6), gradually/little-by-little (2), small in worth (4), rhetorical המעט (7). Rich adverbial polysemy confirmed by distinct glosses across languages."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5012","glossChirho":"נבא prophesy","fromChirho":41,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Nifal 'prophesy, deliver oracle' (24) vs Hitpael 'prophesy ecstatically' (17). The binyan split corresponds to a real semantic distinction: authoritative prophetic speech vs Spirit-compelled ecstatic prophecy (1 Sam 10, 19). Arabic تنبأ is uniform but Korean 예언하다 vs 신들려 예언하다 hints at distinction."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5193","glossChirho":"נטע plant","fromChirho":41,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Literal plant vegetation (32) vs metaphorical establish people in land (7, divine planting) vs pitch/fix non-vegetative objects (2, tent-pegs/nails — Hindi गाड़ेगा, Arabic ينصب). The plant-people metaphor is well-attested in BDB."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6113","glossChirho":"עצר shut, restrain","fromChirho":41,"toChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Seven senses across stems: shut/close (7), restrain/hold back (8), Nifal be stopped (5), confined/imprisoned passive ptc (10), detain (3), retain/keep possession (5), idiomatic 'have power/be able' (3). The imprisoned participle is the largest single category."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6607","glossChirho":"פֶּתַח entrance","fromChirho":41,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Entrance/opening/doorway (29) vs door/gate as physical structure (12). Arabic مدخل (place of entering) vs باب (door), Spanish entrada vs puerta clearly separate. The entrance is the space, the door is the closure."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6817","glossChirho":"צעק cry out","fromChirho":41,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Qal/Piel cry out in distress (35) vs Nifal passive 'be summoned/assembled' (5, Spanish convocados, Korean 소집되었다) vs Hifil causative 'summon/call together' (1). Clean binyan-driven split — the Nifal assembly sense is semantically quite distant from crying."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7223","glossChirho":"רִאשׁוֹן first","fromChirho":41,"toChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Eight senses: ordinal first (11), former/previous (9), former things prophetic (2, Isaiah substantive), at first/beginning temporal (5), as at the first comparative (5), former ones/ancestors (7), chief/foremost rank (1), from the beginning origin (1). Rich adjectival polysemy between temporal, ordinal, and rank meanings."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7368","glossChirho":"רחק be far","fromChirho":41,"toChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Seven senses across stems: Qal stative be-far (10), Qal active go-far-away (12), Hifil/Piel causative remove-far (14), Piel intensive extend/enlarge (1), Qal inf keep-far-from/abstain (2), prepositional distance-measure (1), Hifil inf-abs adverbial far-off (1). The causative vs stative is the key split."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G3588","glossChirho":"ὁ/ἡ/τό the","fromChirho":40,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Greek article with 6 syntactic functions: definite article (14), substantivizer/pronoun (7), genitive article (6), dative article (9), connective/structural (2), comparative (2). Primarily syntactic rather than semantic polysemy — the article's meaning shifts with case and context."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G3956","glossChirho":"πᾶς all","fromChirho":40,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses: collective plural all (18), distributive every/each (3), whole/entire totality (1), substantive everything (16), temporal always/continually (2, Spanish siempre, Hindi हमेशा). Parallel to Hebrew כֹּל — same function-word polysemy pattern."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G4198","glossChirho":"πορεύομαι go","fromChirho":40,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Physical go/proceed (28) vs journey/travel extended trip (6, Swahili kusafiri) vs figurative walk/conduct oneself (6, Korean 행하다). Very similar split to G4043 περιπατέω — both verbs have the literal/figurative motion dichotomy."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0854","glossChirho":"אֵת with","fromChirho":40,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses: comitative with/accompaniment (14), direct object marker (10), ablative מֵאֵת from-with (10), proximity near/beside (5), adversative against (1). The object marker is a genuine homograph — grammatical particle vs semantic preposition."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2372","glossChirho":"חזה see, behold","fromChirho":40,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses: basic visual perception (24), contemplative gaze (8, Spanish contemplar, Arabic نظر), imperative behold (4), prophetic vision (3), rare grasp/seize (1, Spanish alcanzar, Arabic أمسك). The grasp sense is a fascinating semantic extension — seeing → seizing."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2416e","glossChirho":"חַיִּים life","fromChirho":40,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Nearly monosemous — 38/40 clusters are abstract life/lifespan. Only 2 clusters are substantival 'the living ones' (living people). Hindi जीवितों and Spanish los vivos distinguish the substantive from abstract."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4116a","glossChirho":"מהר hurry","fromChirho":40,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Clean binyan split: Piel active hurry/make haste (36) vs Nifal characterological be-rash/hasty (4). The Nifal describes temperament not action."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5381","glossChirho":"נשׂג overtake","fromChirho":40,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses: overtake in pursuit (24), reach/arrive at (9), afford/suffice Levitical 'hand reaches' (4), obtain blessing (2, Isaiah), rare move/remove (1). The afford/suffice idiom is the most interesting — a hand-reaching metaphor for economic ability."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5930a","glossChirho":"עֹלָה burnt offering","fromChirho":40,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Nearly monosemous — 39/40 clusters are burnt offering/sacrifice. One cluster (124317) is actually עַוְלָה (injustice) — likely a homographic tagging error in source data. Arabic بالظلم, Hindi अधर्म confirm injustice not offering."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G1525","glossChirho":"εἰσέρχομαι enter","fromChirho":39,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Physical enter place (34) vs figurative enter state (4, entering rest/kingdom/life — Swahili kimefikia) vs pass through narrow space (1, camel/needle — Spanish pasar). The state-entry metaphor is theologically significant."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G2198","glossChirho":"ζάω live","fromChirho":39,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Stative be alive (34) vs come back to life/resurrection (2, Swahili amefufuka) vs conduct one's life/live through a period (3, Spanish vivieron). The resurrection sense is theologically distinct — Arabic أحياه vs يحيا confirms."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0413","glossChirho":"אֵל to, toward","fromChirho":39,"toChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Seven prepositional senses: directional to/toward (21), into/entering (4), against/opposition (5), upon/over (3), for/concerning (3), with/among (2), before/in presence (1). Function word with rich spatial polysemy. Arabic إلى vs ضد vs على provides clearest separation."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0990","glossChirho":"בֶּטֶן belly","fromChirho":39,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four senses: physical belly/abdomen (20), womb/place of gestation (10), idiomatic מבטן from-birth/since-the-womb (8), figurative inner being/inmost self (1). The belly vs womb split is the primary axis — Arabic بطن vs رحم clearly separate."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1644","glossChirho":"גרשׁ drive out","fromChirho":39,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four senses: Piel active drive-out/expel (29), Pual/Nifal passive be-driven-out (3), technical legal divorced-woman (4, Qal passive ptc fem), and Nifal toss/surge of turbulent water (3, Isa 57:20). The divorced-woman sense is a fascinating social extension."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1995a","glossChirho":"הָמוֹן multitude","fromChirho":39,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four senses: crowd/multitude of people (19), tumult/uproar/commotion (8), noise/sound/roar (6), abundance/wealth (6). Arabic جمهور vs ضجة vs ضجيج vs وفرة cleanly separates all four."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2583","glossChirho":"חנה encamp","fromChirho":39,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Nearly monosemous — 30/39 clusters are neutral camping. Military encamp-against/besiege (9) confirmed by Arabic حاصر (besiege) vs نزل/خيّم (camp), Hindi घेरो (surround)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2763a","glossChirho":"חרם utterly destroy","fromChirho":39,"toChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Seven senses: Hifil utterly destroy (32), devote/consecrate to God (2, Korean 봉헌하는), dry up/make desolate (1, Arabic يجفف), place under ban/curse (1, Spanish anatematicéis), Hofal be-devoted-to-destruction (1), Hofal be-consecrated (1), Hofal be-confiscated (1, Korean 몰수될것이다). Rich Hifil/Hofal passive splits."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7167","glossChirho":"קרע tear","fromChirho":39,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses: active tear/rend garments (28), stative torn condition (3), Nifal passive be-torn (4), cut-open/split-apart (3, Swahili alikata, Arabic شقّ), metaphorical tear-wide/enlarge-breach (1, Arabic توسّعين)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7563","glossChirho":"רָשָׁע wicked","fromChirho":39,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Nearly monosemous — 36/39 clusters are moral wickedness. Forensic guilty/culpable (3) confirmed by Arabic المذنب (guilty, not شرير wicked), Spanish culpable (not malvado), Swahili mkosa/mwenye hatia. Legal vs moral distinction."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G1831","glossChirho":"ἐξέρχομαι go out","fromChirho":38,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Physical go/come out (30) vs depart/withdraw (2) vs imperative command esp. exorcism 'come out!' (4, Swahili Toka) vs go-forth/spread (2). The exorcism imperative is a distinctive NT usage."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G5342","glossChirho":"φέρω bring, bear","fromChirho":38,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses: bring/fetch (18), carry/transport physically (8), bear fruit/produce (6, Swahili kuzaa, Korean 맺는), endure/tolerate (2, Spanish soportó), drive along/impel passively (4). The fruit-bearing metaphor is the most distinctive."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2320","glossChirho":"חֹדֶשׁ month","fromChirho":38,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Month as calendar period (25) vs new moon as astronomical event/festival (13, Korean 초하루, Arabic رأس الشهر). Clean split confirmed by all languages using distinct terms."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2351","glossChirho":"חוּץ outside","fromChirho":38,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four senses: spatial outside/exterior (19), street/broad-place urban (16, typically plural חוצות), open field/countryside (2), adverbial apart-from/except (1). The street sense using the plural is a genuine distinct meaning."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2470a","glossChirho":"חלה be sick","fromChirho":38,"toChirho":10,"notesChirho":"Ten senses — highest polysemy ratio in this batch. Qal be-sick (12), become-weak (3), Nifal grievous/severe (4), harmful/distressing (2), grieve/suffer-pain (3), be-exhausted (2), Nifal weak (2), Hifil/Piel causative make-sick (5), Hofal wounded (2), Hitpael feign-illness (3). Extremely rich binyan-driven polysemy."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3206","glossChirho":"יֶלֶד child","fromChirho":38,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses: singular child/boy (9), plural children/boys (10), youths/young men older (4, Spanish jovenes, Swahili vijana), possessive one's-children/offspring (11), young of animals (4, Korean 새끼들, Spanish crias). The animal-young sense is clearly distinct."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3332","glossChirho":"יצק pour","fromChirho":38,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four senses: pour out liquid (25), cast molten metal (11, Spanish fundido, Hindi ढाला), metaphorical firm/solid/hardened (1, Job 41 Hofal), set down/place (1, 2 Kgs 4:44). The pour vs cast distinction is the key split — Arabic صب vs سبك."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5048","glossChirho":"נֶגֶד before, opposite","fromChirho":38,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six prepositional senses: before/in-presence (16), opposite/facing (5), from-before/away (7, with min), at-a-distance/from-afar (6), against/opposition (3), corresponding-to/counterpart (1, Gen 2:18 כנגדו). Rich spatial polysemy."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5104","glossChirho":"נָהָר river","fromChirho":38,"toChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Completely monosemous — all 38 clusters are 'river/stream'. No semantic variation across the Euphrates, Nile, cosmic floods, or poetic usage. All languages render uniformly: Arabic نهر, Korean 강, Swahili mto."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6466","glossChirho":"פעל do, make","fromChirho":38,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Substantival participle 'doer/worker of' (8, esp. workers-of-iniquity), general do/perform (21), make/fashion/create (8, God as Maker), labor/toil (1). The doer-of-iniquity participle is a distinctive biblical Hebrew collocation."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6960a","glossChirho":"קוה wait, hope","fromChirho":38,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Wait-for/await (21) vs hope-in/trust-expectantly (10) vs lie-in-wait/ambush (1, Hindi घात लगाए, Arabic تَرَقَّبوا) vs substantival those-who-wait (6, Qal ptc). The wait vs hope distinction is the primary axis — subtle but confirmed by Arabic انتظر vs رجا."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7819a","glossChirho":"שׁחט slaughter","fromChirho":38,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Qal active slaughter (32) vs Nifal passive be-slaughtered (2) vs passive ptc the-slaughtered-one (1) vs active ptc one-who-slaughters (2) vs transferred/metaphorical sharp-for-slaughter (1). Mostly monosemous with participle splits."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G1492","glossChirho":"οἶδα know","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Know facts/truths propositional (23, Spanish saber, Arabic علم) vs know personally/recognize acquaintance (14, Spanish conocer, Arabic عرف). The saber/conocer distinction in Spanish is the clearest discriminator — mirrors the epistemological split perfectly."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G3982","glossChirho":"πείθω persuade","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Active persuade/convince (10) vs passive be-persuaded (12) vs perfect trust/have-confidence (11) vs obey/comply (4). The perfect-tense trust sense is a well-known Greek lexicographic distinction — pepoitha as settled confidence."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0310a","glossChirho":"אַחַר after","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Temporal after/afterward (13, Arabic بعد, Korean 후에) vs spatial behind/in-pursuit (24, Arabic وراء, Korean 뒤에). Clean temporal vs spatial split confirmed by Arabic using entirely different prepositions."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0505","glossChirho":"אֶלֶף thousand","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Cardinal 1000 (14) vs dual 2000 (3) vs plural thousands as large numbers (9) vs clan/tribal subdivision (11, Micah 5:2 'clans of Judah'). The clan sense is a famous crux in Hebrew lexicography — genuinely distinct from the numeral."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0926","glossChirho":"בהל be terrified","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six senses: Nifal be-terrified (22), Nifal be-hasty (2), Piel terrify-others (7), Piel hasten-someone (2), Pual be-hastened (2), Hifil cause-to-hasten (2). Dual semantic fields (terror + haste) × stem distinctions."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1116","glossChirho":"בָּמָה high place","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Cultic high-place/shrine (29, Korean 산당) vs topographical height/elevated terrain (8, poetic Hab 3:19, Deut 32:13). The shrine vs terrain distinction is fundamental to biblical theology — deuteronomistic condemnation applies only to the cultic sense."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1272","glossChirho":"ברח flee","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Qal flee/run-away (30) vs Qal pass-through/traverse (1, Spanish atravesar, Swahili kupitia) vs Hifil drive-away/chase (5) vs Hifil send-through/cause-to-pass (1). The pass-through sense is a known secondary meaning of barach."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2865","glossChirho":"חתת be dismayed","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Qal/Nifal emotional be-dismayed/afraid (24) vs Qal/Nifal physical be-shattered/broken (7, Spanish destrozado, Swahili imevunjwa) vs Hifil/Piel causative terrify (5) vs Hifil causative shatter (1). Dual semantic fields (fear + breaking) confirmed by Arabic خاف vs حطم."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3245","glossChirho":"יסד found, establish","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six senses: Qal active found (14), Qal ptc founding-agent (1), Piel intensive establish (9), Pual/Hofal passive be-founded (9), Nifal passive be-established (2), Nifal reciprocal conspire/take-counsel (2). The conspire sense is a fascinating semantic development from 'establishing together'."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3256","glossChirho":"יסר discipline","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six senses: Piel active discipline/chastise (21), instruct/teach/train (5), Nifal passive be-disciplined (3), Nifal reflexive be-warned (3), Qal active admonish/reprove (3), Hifil bind/chastise (2, Hosea). Discipline vs instruction is the key split."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3701","glossChirho":"כֶּסֶף silver","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Silver the metal (30) vs money/currency/purchase-price (7, Korean 돈 vs 은, Hindi धन vs चाँदी, Spanish dinero vs plata). Clean metonymic split confirmed by distinct lexemes in every language."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3754","glossChirho":"כֶּרֶם vineyard","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Completely monosemous — all 37 clusters are vineyard. No semantic variation across any grammatical form. Arabic كرم, Korean 포도원, Swahili shamba la mizabibu all uniform."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4886","glossChirho":"משׁח anoint","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Clean sacral/profane split: ceremonial anointing (kings, priests, objects) vs passive be-anointed vs cosmetic smearing. Arabic مسح covers broader wiping/anointing but the sacral sense is distinctly marked across languages."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5062","glossChirho":"נגף strike","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Neat three-way: active strike/smite, Nifal military defeat/rout, and stumble/dash-against (physical/moral tripping). Korean 치다 vs 패하다 vs 걸리다 confirm the tripartite split."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5234a","glossChirho":"נכר recognize","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Rich polysemy: recognize/identify, acknowledge formally, show partiality (with פנים), Hitpael make-oneself-known, Nifal be-recognized, privative Piel deny/disown, Piel passive be-favored. The privative Piel (anti-recognize = deny) is a textbook Hebrew semantic pattern."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6299","glossChirho":"פדה redeem","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Full binyan paradigm: Qal active redeem, Qal passive ptc redeemed-ones, Qal active ptc redeemer, Nifal be-redeemed, Hifil causative, Hofal passive-causative. Arabic فدى confirms the core ransom sense."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7617","glossChirho":"שׁבה take captive","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Active/passive/agentive/patient quaternary: take captive, be taken captive, captor (active ptc), captive (passive ptc). Arabic سبى tracks perfectly. Clean participial substantivization."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7703","glossChirho":"שׁדד destroy","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six-way including agentive destroyer (Qal ptc), active destroy (Qal finite), intensive devastate (Piel), passive be-laid-waste (Pual), passive be-destroyed (Qal pass/Nifal), and inf-abs adverbial utterly. The Piel/Pual intensive pair is well attested."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7969","glossChirho":"שָׁלֹשׁ three","fromChirho":37,"toChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Completely monosemous cardinal numeral. All 37 senses collapse to one — numerals have no polysemy, only morphological variation (construct, suffix, compound)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G0154","glossChirho":"αἰτέω ask","fromChirho":36,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Binary: humble request/petition vs authoritative demand/require. Spanish pedir vs exigir, Arabic طلب vs مطالبة confirm the register distinction. Most occurrences are the petition sense."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G1223","glossChirho":"διά through","fromChirho":36,"toChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Seven preposition senses: spatial through, causal because-of, interrogative why (διὰ τί), inferential therefore, temporal during, purposive for-sake-of, temporal after. Greek prepositions are notoriously polysemous but multilingual data separates them cleanly."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0215","glossChirho":"אור shine (verb)","fromChirho":36,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five-way by binyan: Qal intransitive shine, Nifal be-illuminated/glorious, Hifil give-light (natural), Hifil make-face-shine (priestly blessing idiom), Hifil kindle (physical fire/lamp). The priestly blessing usage is a frozen idiom requiring its own sense."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0216","glossChirho":"אוֹר light (noun)","fromChirho":36,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four nominal senses: general/metaphorical light (32 of 36 occurrences — dominant), lightning flash, dawn/morning-light, and plural luminaries. The metaphorical extension (life, favor, guidance) is so pervasive it doesn't warrant splitting from physical light."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0389","glossChirho":"אַךְ only/surely/but","fromChirho":36,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Classic Hebrew particle trichotomy: restrictive only/merely, asseverative surely/indeed, adversative but/however. All three are well-established in BDB. Distribution is roughly even (9/13/14)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1523","glossChirho":"גיל rejoice","fromChirho":36,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Overwhelmingly monosemous rejoice/be-glad (33 of 36). Two minor senses: tremble-with-awe (Ps 2:11 where joy borders on trembling) and nominal/adverbial infinitive-absolute use. Arabic جال (be agitated) preserves the original physical movement sense."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1580","glossChirho":"גמל deal/wean","fromChirho":36,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four-way: wean a child (physical nurturing to completion), deal bountifully (generous action), repay/recompense/requite (reciprocal dealing — largest group at 18), ripen/bear fruit (agricultural). The semantic thread is 'bring to completion/maturity' across all senses."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1589","glossChirho":"גנב steal","fromChirho":36,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses: steal property (Qal), kidnap person (Qal/Piel with personal object), thief (participial), be stolen (passive Nifal/Pual), steal away/sneak (Hitpael reflexive). Arabic سرق vs خطف (kidnap) confirms the property/person distinction."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3637","glossChirho":"כלם be ashamed","fromChirho":36,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Clean binyan triad: Nifal be-ashamed/disgraced (23), Hifil put-to-shame/humiliate (10), Hofal be-humiliated-by-another (3). The Hofal passive-of-causative neatly distinguishes inflicted shame from experienced shame."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4421","glossChirho":"מִלְחָמָה war","fromChirho":36,"toChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Completely monosemous concrete noun. All 36 senses collapse to armed conflict — no metaphorical or extended usages warranting separate senses."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5128","glossChirho":"נוע shake/wander","fromChirho":36,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Rich polysemy: wander-as-fugitive, Hifil cause-to-wander/scatter, sway/stagger/totter, tremble-from-fear, Hifil transitive shake/wag (head in scorn), sift-as-sieve. Arabic نوع preserves the core oscillation/movement sense."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6419","glossChirho":"פלל pray","fromChirho":36,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Hitpael pray dominates (32 of 36). Two rare Piel senses: judge/intercede (3) and think/suppose (1). The Hitpael reflexive 'intercede for oneself before God' became the standard prayer verb — fascinating semantic specialization."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7264","glossChirho":"רגז tremble","fromChirho":36,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six-way: Qal tremble/quake (23), Qal rage/be-agitated (3), Qal quarrel (1), Hitpael rage-intensely (2), Hifil cause-to-tremble (4), Hifil disturb/provoke (3). The physical-trembling to emotional-rage spectrum is well attested across Semitic."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7965","glossChirho":"שָׁלוֹם peace","fromChirho":36,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six senses: peace/no-conflict (19), welfare/well-being (6), greeting-formula (5), friend/ally (3), adverbial completeness (1), proper-name Salem (2). Arabic سلام confirms the peace/greeting/well-being triad. The proper-name sense is notable for lexicography."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H8147","glossChirho":"שְׁנַיִם two","fromChirho":36,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four numeral senses: cardinal two (18), pronominal both-of-them (10), compound twelve (7), multiplicative double/twofold (1). Unlike שָׁלֹשׁ (three→1), שְׁנַיִם develops distinct pronominal and multiplicative uses."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H8597","glossChirho":"תִּפְאֶרֶת glory/beauty","fromChirho":36,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three-way: abstract glory/honor/majesty (19), aesthetic beauty/splendor (14), concrete ornament/finery (3). The abstract→concrete gradient is clean: renown → visual magnificence → wearable decoration."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G0591","glossChirho":"ἀποδίδωμι repay","fromChirho":35,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six senses: repay-debt (6), reward/recompense (10), give-back/return (7), give/deliver/render (7), yield-fruit (2), sell/exchange (3). The ἀπο- prefix drives the 'back/away' semantics across all senses."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G0846","glossChirho":"αὐτός self/he","fromChirho":35,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three pronoun functions: 3rd-person reference he/she/it/they (28), intensive-reflexive himself/themselves (2), article+αὐτός = the-same/identical (5). Classic Greek grammar distinction."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G2212","glossChirho":"ζητέω seek","fromChirho":35,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three-way: physical seek/search-for (18), abstract desire/strive-to-obtain (14), passive be-required/demanded (3). Spanish buscar vs procurar vs ser requerido maps cleanly."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G3140","glossChirho":"μαρτυρέω testify","fromChirho":35,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Binary active/passive: testify/bear-witness (25) vs be-attested/receive-testimony (10). The passive is well-represented — Hebrews 11 'received testimony' uses this consistently."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1992","glossChirho":"הֵם they","fromChirho":35,"toChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Eight senses by prepositional combination: independent they (11), demonstrative those (2), dative to/for-them (8), reflexive for-themselves (3), separative from-them (4), comparative like-them (3), locative in/with-them (2), 3fs variant (2). High count reflects genuine functional differences with prepositions."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2346","glossChirho":"חוֹמָה wall","fromChirho":35,"toChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Completely monosemous concrete noun. All 35 senses = city fortification wall. No metaphorical or extended usages in the corpus."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2734","glossChirho":"חרה be angry","fromChirho":35,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses: Qal anger-was-kindled (30, overwhelmingly dominant idiom חָרָה אַף), Hitpael fret-oneself (2), Nifal-ptc those-incensed (1), Hifil kindle-fire (1), Hifil burn-with-zeal (1). The חָרָה אַף idiom accounts for 86% of occurrences."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4438","glossChirho":"מַלְכוּת kingdom","fromChirho":35,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three-way: kingdom/realm as territory (24), reign/rule as temporal exercise (8), royalty/dignity as abstract quality (3). Arabic مملكة vs حكم vs ملكية maps well."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4720","glossChirho":"מִקְדָּשׁ sanctuary","fromChirho":35,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three senses: singular sanctuary/holy-place (26), plural sanctuaries/precincts (8), consecrated-portion/sacred-thing (1). The singular dominates — mostly refers to the tabernacle/temple."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4908","glossChirho":"מִשְׁכָּן tabernacle/dwelling","fromChirho":35,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Binary: technical Tabernacle (9 Exodus-Numbers cultic uses) vs general dwelling/habitation (26). The technical sense is a semantic specialization of the general 'dwelling' root."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6041","glossChirho":"עָנִי poor/afflicted","fromChirho":35,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four-way: materially poor (19), afflicted/oppressed (12), humble/lowly in spirit (2), feminine personification of suffering city (2). The poor→afflicted→humble gradient shows the full semantic range in OT poverty theology."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6293","glossChirho":"פגע meet/encounter","fromChirho":35,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses: meet/encounter (11), reach boundary (2), strike/attack (10), intercede/entreat (10), Hifil lay-upon (2). The meet→strike and meet→intercede extensions from the core 'encounter' sense are both well attested."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6565a","glossChirho":"פרר break/annul","fromChirho":35,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five-way: break covenant (13), annul vow (11), frustrate counsel (6), be broken passive (4), shatter physically Piel (1). Arabic نقض (break covenant) vs أبطل (annul) confirms the first two as distinct."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7561","glossChirho":"רשׁע be wicked","fromChirho":35,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four senses: Qal be-wicked/guilty (8), Hifil declarative condemn/declare-guilty (17), Hifil elative act-wickedly (9), adjective wicked (1). The Hifil has two distinct uses: declarative (forensic) vs elative (behavioral)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7891","glossChirho":"שׁיר sing","fromChirho":35,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three-way: sing verb (17), singer/temple-musician participle (17), be sung Hofal (1). The participial singer sense is exactly half — showing how important the Levitical singer role is in the corpus."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H8441","glossChirho":"תּוֹעֵבָה abomination","fromChirho":35,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Binary singular/plural: singular abomination (8) vs plural abominations (27). The plural dominates — Ezekiel alone accounts for much of the plural usage cataloging detestable practices."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G2065","glossChirho":"ἐρωτάω ask","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Binary: ask/inquire/question (15) vs request/beseech/entreat (19). In Classical Greek ἐρωτάω = 'ask a question' only, but NT usage expands to include requests — a well-known NT semantic shift."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G2902","glossChirho":"κρατέω seize","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six senses: seize/arrest (13), grasp/take-hold (4), hold-fast/retain (11), be-held/restrained passive (3), attain/obtain figurative (1), keep/observe traditions (2). The physical→abstract gradient is clean."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G3089","glossChirho":"λύω loose","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six NT senses: untie physically (13), release/set-free (9), break/violate law (4), destroy/tear-down (4), dissolve/melt (3), dismiss assembly (1). The core 'unbind' sense generates all extensions through metaphor."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0518b","glossChirho":"אִם if","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six particle senses: conditional if (5), restrictive only/except (16, largest!), asseverative surely (3), adversative but/rather (8), concessive even-if (1), temporal when (1). The restrictive sense outweighs the conditional — interesting for a word glossed primarily as 'if'."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0734","glossChirho":"אֹרַח path","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six senses: physical path/road (15), moral way-of-life (15), traveler metonymy (1), caravan (1), manner/custom (1), course/walk-of-life (1). The concrete/figurative split is perfectly balanced at 15/15."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0959","glossChirho":"בזה despise","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three-way by binyan: active despise Qal (25), passive/stative despised Nifal/Qal-pass (8), causative cause-to-be-despised Hifil (1). Arabic احتقر (despise) vs مُحتقَر (despised) confirms the active/passive split."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2040","glossChirho":"הרס tear down","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four-way: Qal active tear-down/demolish (20), Nifal passive be-torn-down (9), Piel intensive destroy-thoroughly (3), break-through/burst-in (2). The break-through sense is a genuine semantic extension beyond mere demolition."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2063","glossChirho":"זֹאת this (fem)","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six prepositional combinations: bare demonstrative this (9), conjunctive and-this (2), instrumental in/by/with-this (9), comparative like-this/thus (9), directional to/for-this (4), comparative than-this (1). Like H1992 (הֵם), prepositional phrases create genuine functional distinctions."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2691a","glossChirho":"חָצֵר court","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Binary: court/courtyard/enclosure (32) vs village/unwalled-settlement (2). The village sense is rare but etymologically interesting — an enclosed space that becomes a small settlement."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3046","glossChirho":"ידע know (Aramaic)","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four senses: Qal know/perceive (14), Qal passive be-known (2), Hifil make-known/declare (17), Hifil teach/instruct (1). The Hifil dominates — Aramaic Daniel heavily uses the causative 'make known' in interpretation contexts."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3117","glossChirho":"יוֹם day","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses: day/specific-time (17), daylight/daytime (2), one's-days/lifetime (9), comparative like-the-days-of (4), dual two-days (2). The lifetime sense is well-attested in formulaic expressions."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3477","glossChirho":"יָשָׁר upright","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six senses: upright/righteous person (22), right/correct action (7), straight/level path (1), proper-name Yashar (1), abstract uprightness (2), substantive upright-things (1). The person sense overwhelmingly dominates."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4100","glossChirho":"מָה what","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Eight interrogative/exclamatory functions: what (6), why (2), how/manner (9), how!/exclamatory (3), how-many/much (5), instrumental in/by-what (6), when (2), indefinite whatever (1). Hebrew מָה is a Swiss-army interrogative — context determines function."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4284","glossChirho":"מַחֲשָׁבָה thought","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four-way: thoughts/mental-reflections (17), plans/purposes (8), schemes/evil-plots (5), inventive-design/skilled-craft (4). The neutral→negative→positive gradient is interesting — same root covers divine planning, human scheming, and artistic craftsmanship."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4904","glossChirho":"מִשְׁכָּב bed","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four senses: bed/couch furniture (25), bedroom/sleeping-chamber metonymy (2), sexual-intercourse euphemism (4), abstract act-of-lying-down (3). The euphemistic 'lyings of' (mishkeve) is a well-known Hebrew construction."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4931","glossChirho":"מִשְׁמֶרֶת charge/duty","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six senses: assigned duty/charge (18, Levitical), watch/shift/rotation (5), guard-post/station (2), commandment/ordinance (5), custody/safekeeping (3), guards/watchmen metonymy (1). Rich semantic field around the root שׁמר (guard)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5483b","glossChirho":"סוּס horse","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Essentially monosemous: horse the animal (33) + 1 parashah section marker (non-lexical). The section marker is a data artifact, not a real sense."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5564","glossChirho":"סמך lay hands/support","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six senses: lay-hands-on ritual (9), sustain/support person (7), one-who-upholds ptc (6), lean-on/rely-upon Nifal (7), press-upon/lie-heavy (2), firm/established passive-ptc (3). Beautiful semantic web from physical laying-on to metaphorical support."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5749b","glossChirho":"עוד warn/testify","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three senses: warn/admonish/charge (18), testify/bear-witness (11), call/summon-as-witness Hifil (5). The warn→testify connection runs through the idea of solemn declaration."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6584","glossChirho":"פשׁט strip/raid","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six senses: Qal strip-off-garments reflexive (7), Qal raid/rush-upon military (11), Qal strip/plunder general (1), Piel strip/despoil intensive (1), Hifil strip-clothing-from-another (10), Hifil flay/skin-animal cultic (4). The strip→raid semantic connection is through 'rush in and strip'."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6663","glossChirho":"צדק be righteous","fromChirho":34,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Full binyan paradigm: Qal be-righteous (16), Piel justify/declare-righteous (5), Hifil declare-righteous/acquit forensic (10), Hifil lead-to-righteousness factitive (1), Hitpael justify-oneself (1), Nifal be-vindicated (1). The Qal/Piel/Hifil triad is theologically significant for 'justification' doctrine."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G3844","glossChirho":"παρά beside/from","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":9,"notesChirho":"Highly polysemous Greek preposition with 9 distinct senses governed by case: genitive (from/beside), dative (with/before/in-sight-of), accusative (alongside/beyond/against). Also comparative (than) and causal (because-of) uses. Case-governed prepositions naturally resist collapse."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0914","glossChirho":"בדל separate","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Clean binyan split: Hifil active separate (divide peoples, set apart), Hifil distinguish/select (choosing Levites, discerning clean/unclean), Nifal reflexive/passive be-separated. The Hifil distinguish sense is subtly different from physical separation — epistemic rather than spatial."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1254a","glossChirho":"ברא create","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Theological keystone verb. Qal create (divine creative acts — heavens, humanity, new things), Qal participle Creator (substantivized title), Nifal passive be-created, and surprising Piel cut-down/clear (forests) — an entirely different semantic field in Piel stem."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2790b","glossChirho":"חרשׁ be silent","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three clear senses: Hifil be/keep silent (dominant, 31 senses), Hifil causative cause-silence/silence-someone (1), and Hitpael show-deafness/be-deaf (1). The overwhelming clustering into 'be silent' reflects genuinely monosemous usage in most contexts."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3444","glossChirho":"יְשׁוּעָה salvation","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Abstract vs concrete plural: singular/abstract salvation/deliverance (29) versus plural victories/acts-of-deliverance (4). The plural concretizes the abstract into specific historical events. Classic Hebrew abstract-plural polysemy pattern."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4186","glossChirho":"מוֹשָׁב dwelling place","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Nominal moshab pattern: dwelling-place/settlement (22 dominant), seat/throne/assembly-seat (9), inhabitants metonymy (1), site/location (1). The seat sense derives from 'sitting' root yashab, while dwelling derives from the habitative extension."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4399","glossChirho":"מְלָאכָה work","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Work taxonomy: general work/labor (27), specialized craftsmanship/artisan-work (3 — temple construction), business/occupation (1), property/goods metonymy (1), livestock as productive-property (1). The craftsmanship sense is contextually bound to temple/tabernacle building narratives."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4900","glossChirho":"משׁך draw/pull","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":9,"notesChirho":"Remarkably polysemous verb with 9 senses spanning physical (draw-out, drag-forcibly), figurative (attract-people, extend/prolong), passive (be-prolonged/deferred), descriptive (tall/stretched Pual), specialized (prolong-horn-blast, wield-weapon, sow-seed). The sow-seed sense is surprising — possibly a different root homograph."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5203","glossChirho":"נטשׁ abandon","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six senses from a core meaning of releasing/letting-go: abandon/forsake (20 dominant), spread-out/scatter (4), cast-down/hurl (4), drawn/unsheathed weapon (1 — releasing from scabbard), become-slack/loose (1), leave/let-alone permissive (3). Physical release → emotional abandonment metaphor chain."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5291","glossChirho":"נַעֲרָה young woman","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Social-status polysemy: young-woman/girl (17 — age-based, no servitude), female-attendant/maidservant (14 — with possessive suffixes indicating belonging), young-women as group (2 — absolute plural). The servant sense is marked by possessive morphology, showing how syntax disambiguates."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5612a","glossChirho":"סֵפֶר book/scroll","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Writing-medium taxonomy: book/scroll as literary work (17), letter/epistle (10), official-document/certificate such as divorce certificate (5), writing/script as material (1). The letter vs book distinction reflects genre and format differences in ancient writing."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6430","glossChirho":"פְּלִשְׁתִּי Philistine","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Proper noun with collective vs individual distinction: Philistines as a people/nation (31 — with article or in construct chains), individual Philistine warrior (2 — David's combat with 'the Philistine'). Near-monosemous — the distinction is grammatical number/reference more than semantic."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6743b","glossChirho":"צלח prosper","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Clean voice/transitivity split: prosper/succeed intransitively (20 — subject thrives), cause-to-prosper causative Hifil (11 — God grants success), be-fit/useful Qal (2 — entirely different semantic frame of suitability). The Qal fitness sense may be a separate root."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6913","glossChirho":"קֶבֶר grave/tomb","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Completely monosemous concrete noun. All 33 occurrences mean grave/tomb/burial-place without semantic variation. Prepositions, pronominal suffixes, and number inflection do not create new senses. A model case of genuine monosemy for a physical-object noun."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7138","glossChirho":"קָרוֹב near","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four-way polysemy along classic axes: spatial nearness (19), temporal nearness/imminence (5), relational nearness as kinsman/neighbor (7 substantivized), cultic nearness of drawing-near-to-God (2 — priestly/worship context). The spatial→temporal→relational→theological metaphor chain is a textbook example of semantic extension."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7181","glossChirho":"קשׁב pay attention","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Near-monosemous verb: Hifil finite pay-attention/heed (29), Hifil participle attentive-one (3), and rare Qal intransitive listen (1). The participle substantivization is the only real polysemy — the verb is semantically uniform across all contexts."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7321","glossChirho":"רוע shout","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses along context-of-shouting axis: joyful worship shout (10), general loud cry (14 — war, distress, vocalization), alarm signal with trumpets (5 — Numbers 10 military), Hitpael triumphant exultation (3), and rare Pual passive be-shouted-over (1). Binyan distinguishes Hitpael reflexive triumph from Hifil transitive."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7355","glossChirho":"רחם have compassion","fromChirho":33,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Piel dominant have-compassion (26), participle compassionate-one (3), Pual passive be-shown-mercy (2), Hosea's symbolic name Ruhamah (1), and distinct Qal love (1). The Ruhamah proper-name sense is a hapax-driven split. Qal 'love' is semantically distinct from Piel 'show compassion'."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G0302","glossChirho":"ἄν particle","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Greek modal particle with 5 syntactic-semantic functions: indefinite generalizer with relatives (11 — whoever/whatever), counterfactual conditional (6), potential/dubitative (9), temporal contingency with temporal conjunctions (3), and subjunctive clause marker (3). A grammar-driven particle whose 'senses' are really syntactic constructions."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G0649","glossChirho":"ἀποστέλλω send","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three senses: active send-on-mission/commission (21 — the apostolic core meaning), passive be-sent/commissioned (9), and send-away/dismiss (2 — emphasis on departure not mission). The active/passive voice split is the primary axis; the dismiss sense has different semantic frame."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G0907","glossChirho":"βαπτίζω baptize","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Active baptize/administer (11), passive be-baptized/receive (19 — dominant, reflecting the perspective of the baptizand), and broader Hellenistic wash/immerse-ritually (2 — pre-Christian ceremonial washing). The active/passive split is voice-driven; the ritual-wash sense preserves the pre-technical meaning."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G1909","glossChirho":"ἐπί upon","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":12,"notesChirho":"Highly polysemous Greek preposition with 12 case-governed senses: spatial-surface on/upon (8), directional to/toward (3), hostile against (1), temporal during (6), purpose for (1), reference about (2), non-surface locative in/at (3), causal basis on-account-of (3), proximity before (2), proportional inasmuch-as (1), sequential after (1), source from (1). Case governs meaning: gen=source, dat=location, acc=direction."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G4095","glossChirho":"πίνω drink","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Completely monosemous — all 32 occurrences are 'drink' whether literal (water, wine) or figurative (cup of suffering, living water, Christ's blood). The figurative uses are metaphorical extensions of a single sense, not distinct senses. Good test case for literal-vs-figurative monosemy."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G4238","glossChirho":"πράσσω do/practice","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four senses: habitual practice (13 — moral pattern of behavior), punctiliar do/perform (15 — specific actions), collect/exact-payment (2 — specialized commercial), passive be-done (2). The practice-vs-perform split is aspectual: iterative habitual vs perfective single-event. The tax-collection sense is a distinct lexicalized meaning."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0702","glossChirho":"אַרְבַּע four","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Two senses: cardinal four (20) and compound fourteen with -asar (12). Similar to H7969 שָׁלֹשׁ pattern — numerals split between standalone and compound forms. Near-monosemous since both are fundamentally 'four'."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0898","glossChirho":"בגד deal treacherously","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Clean verb/substantive split: deal-treacherously finite (17) versus treacherous-person participle (15). The participle is so heavily used substantively (15 of 32 occurrences!) that it functions as a near-independent noun. Classic Hebrew participle-as-agent-noun pattern."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0962","glossChirho":"בזז plunder","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four senses: Qal active plunder (22), Qal participle plunderer (5), Nifal/Piel passive be-plundered (4), Qal passive participle plundered-state (1). A voice-and-morphology-driven four-way split: active verb, agent noun, passive process, resultative state."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1964","glossChirho":"הֵיכָל temple/palace","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Classic polysemy: temple/sacred-building (22) versus palace/royal-residence (10). The Akkadian loanword (ekallu) carries both meanings. Context determines which — divine reference = temple, royal reference = palace. A building-type polysemy, not metaphor."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3320","glossChirho":"יצב stand/station","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five stance-related senses: physical stand/take-position (11), present-before-authority (8 — formal court/divine), station-deliberately for purpose (6), stand-firm/withstand (3 — resistance), take-stand-collectively (4 — communal/ceremonial). The present-before-authority and stand-firm senses have distinct pragmatic frames."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3557","glossChirho":"כול contain/sustain","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses from a core 'hold/contain' meaning: physical contain/hold-capacity (10), sustain/provide-for/nourish (15 — dominant, Piel provision), Pual passive be-provisioned (1), psychological endure/bear/withstand (5), and conduct/manage-affairs (1). The physical→provision→endurance metaphor chain is clear."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3678","glossChirho":"כִּסֵּא throne","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three senses: royal/divine throne (25 dominant), ordinary seat/chair (4), and plural thrones as seats of authority for subordinate rulers/judges (3). The throne-vs-seat split is contextual: divine/royal context = throne, ordinary context = chair."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3766","glossChirho":"כרע bow down","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four senses: worship/submission bow (22 dominant), animal crouch (2 — lion imagery), physical collapse of knees from weakness (2), and Hifil causative bring-down/subdue (6). The Hifil causative is the clearest binyan-driven sense split."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4347","glossChirho":"מַכָּה blow/wound","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four-way concrete→abstract chain: physical blow/strike (9), resulting wound/injury (15 dominant), divine plague/calamity (6 — God as agent of striking), and military slaughter/defeat (2). Metonymy drives cause→effect→judgment progression."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4467","glossChirho":"מַמְלָכָה kingdom","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Near-monosemous: concrete kingdom/realm/territory (30) versus abstract kingship/royal-dominion (2). The vast majority of uses refer to territorial kingdoms. Classic concrete-abstract polysemy."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4581","glossChirho":"מָעוֹז fortress","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three senses: literal military fortress/stronghold (21 dominant), metaphorical refuge/safety especially of God (9), and abstract strength/protection (2). Concrete fortress → divine metaphor → abstract quality progression."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4784","glossChirho":"מרה rebel","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses: Qal rebel (12), Qal participle rebellious-one (3), Hifil provoke-rebellion (13), Hifil participle rebellious-ones (1), and stative bitter/bitterness (3). The rebel/bitter polysemy reflects the etymological connection — rebellion as 'bitterness' against authority. Both Qal and Hifil have participle substantivizations."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6471","glossChirho":"פַּעַם time/foot","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four quite distinct senses: time/occasion for counting (17 — 'seven times'), foot/base physical (6), step/footstep figurative for conduct (8), and hapax anvil in Isa 41:7 (1). The time/foot homophony may reflect two originally distinct roots merged under one lemma."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6845","glossChirho":"צפן hide/treasure","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Seven senses from a rich semantic field: Qal hide (7), Nifal be-hidden (3), Hifil cause-to-hide (2), treasure-up/store-away (9), lurk/lie-in-ambush (4), shelter/protect (1), and Qal passive participle treasure-as-substantive (6). The hide→treasure→lurk extension chain shows how a single root develops hostile and protective specializations."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7522","glossChirho":"רָצוֹן favor/will","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four senses: favor/goodwill (15 dominant), cultic acceptance-of-offering (6), will/desire/purpose (9), and delight/pleasure (2). The favor→acceptance and will→delight pairs show the noun's semantic range spanning relational, ritual, volitional, and emotional domains."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H8040","glossChirho":"שְׂמֹאול left","fromChirho":32,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three senses: left-side/direction (25 dominant), left-hand body-part (6), and north (1 — from east-facing orientation convention). The north sense is a cultural-geographic extension unique to ANE spatial orientation."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G0305","glossChirho":"ἀναβαίνω go up","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses: physical ascend/go-up (24 dominant — especially to Jerusalem), board/embark ship (1), plants grow-up (2), abstract rise-up/come-to-mind (1), and theological ascend-to-heaven (3 — Christ's ascension). The heavenly ascension is theologically specialized beyond mere physical going-up."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G1125","glossChirho":"γράφω write","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four senses: formulaic γέγραπται 'it-is-written' Scripture citation (2), written/recorded state (9), active write/compose documents (18 dominant), and passive be-written (2). The γέγραπται formula is so crystallized in NT usage as a divine-authority appeal that it warrants its own sense."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G1392","glossChirho":"δοξάζω glorify","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four voice-driven senses: active glorify/praise (20 dominant), passive be-glorified (8), reflexive glorify-oneself (2 — negative connotation), and stative glorified/glorious (1). The reflexive sense carries distinct pejorative force compared to the transitive praise."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G2334","glossChirho":"θεωρέω see/watch","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three senses along an attention-intensity axis: basic see/perceive (17), sustained watch/gaze-at (13), and consider/contemplate (1). The verb inherently implies more attentive seeing than ὁράω, making the contemplative sense a natural extension."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G4052","glossChirho":"περισσεύω abound","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four senses: intransitive abound/overflow (20 dominant), remain-over/surplus (6), causative cause-to-abound (3), and passive be-given-abundance (2). The remain-over sense is semantically distinct — surplus after satiation vs general abundance."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G5319","glossChirho":"φανερόω make known/manifest","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three senses: active make-known/reveal (7), personal appear/be-manifested especially of Christ's incarnation and parousia (9), and passive of-things be-revealed/disclosed (15). The person-vs-thing subject distinction in the passive is semantically significant — Christological manifestation is a distinct frame."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0319","glossChirho":"אַחֲרִית end/latter","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six senses from a core 'what-comes-after' concept: latter-period/eschatological (6), outcome/fate (16 dominant), future/hope (3 — Jer 29:11), posterity/descendants (3), remnant/survivors (2), and spatial extremity (1). The outcome/fate dominance shows the word's primary use in wisdom and prophetic discourse."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0637","glossChirho":"אַף also/indeed","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":7,"notesChirho":"Seven discourse-particle functions: additive also/too (7), emphatic indeed (6), a-fortiori how-much-more (7), scalar even (4), connective moreover (2), interrogative-emphatic (4), and rare adversative yet (1). A rich particle whose 'senses' are really pragmatic functions determined by clause position and co-text."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0657a","glossChirho":"אֶפֶס ends/nothing","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses: ends-of-earth (9 — geographic extremity), nothingness (3), there-is-none/no-one (8 — existential negation), adversative however/but/only (5), and privative without/for-lack-of (6). From 'cessation/end' → 'extremity' and 'nothingness' → 'there is not' — a negation particle derived from a concrete noun."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1361","glossChirho":"גבה be high","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses along height axes: physical height (6), honor/exaltation positive (8), haughtiness negative (7 — lifted-up heart), Hifil causative make-high/exalt (7), and specialized soar/fly of eagles (3). The positive exaltation vs negative haughtiness split is evaluative — same metaphor, opposite valence."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H1817c","glossChirho":"דֶּלֶת door","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Near-monosemous: door/gate (29 dominant), column-of-writing (1 — Jer 36:23 hapax, from door-leaf shape of scroll columns), lid/cover (1). The scroll-column sense is a fascinating metonymy from the physical shape of scroll panels resembling door-leaves."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2219","glossChirho":"זרה scatter/winnow","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Six senses: scatter/disperse people (16 dominant — divine judgment), winnow grain literal (7), winnow/examine/sift metaphorical (1 — Ps 139:3), spread/scatter things (3), passive be-scattered (3), and cast-away/fling (1). The scatter-people and winnow-grain senses may reflect separate but merged roots."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2296","glossChirho":"חגר gird","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three senses: active gird-on/fasten (25 dominant — sword, sackcloth, ephod), stative girded/wearing (5 — participial), and distinct homographic root tremble/shudder (1 — חגר II in prophetic texts). The homograph identification is a genuine root-level disambiguation."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2450","glossChirho":"חָכָם wise","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Four senses: adjectival wise/prudent (10), substantivized wise-men/sages as social class (16 dominant — court advisors), skilled/expert-craftsman (3 — Exodus tabernacle), and feminine wise-women (2). The sage-as-class sense is the most frequent, reflecting Wisdom Literature's institutional framing."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2543","glossChirho":"חֲמוֹר donkey","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Completely monosemous concrete animal noun. All 31 occurrences refer to the male domesticated donkey with zero semantic variation. Another model case of genuine monosemy alongside H6913 קֶבֶר (grave) and G4095 πίνω (drink)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3373","glossChirho":"יָרֵא fearing","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses: God-fearing singular (8 — pious reverence), those-who-fear plural (9), dative to/for-those-who-fear (4 — same sense with lamed), afraid/fearful emotional terror (8), and nominal fear/reverence (2). The reverence-vs-terror distinction is the primary semantic axis — same root, different pragmatic frames."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3426","glossChirho":"יֵשׁ there is","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three senses: existential there-is/are (22 dominant), copulative with pronoun suffixes you-are/he-is (8), and rare nominalized substance/wealth (1 — Prov 8:21). The copulative use with suffixes is syntactically distinct from the bare existential particle."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4229a","glossChirho":"מחה blot out/wipe","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"Five senses: blot-out/erase records/sins (14 dominant), physical wipe/wipe-away (5 — tears, dishes), wipe-out/destroy living beings (2 — Flood), Nifal passive be-blotted-out (7), and Hifil causative destroy (3). The erase→wipe→destroy spectrum shows increasing intensity from records to persons."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4975","glossChirho":"מׇתְנַיִם loins","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Two senses: body-part loins/waist (27 dominant — girding, pain, trembling) versus procreative loins as source-of-descendants (4 — 'from his loins'). The procreative metonymy is a distinct semantic frame even though the referent is the same body part."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5045","glossChirho":"נֶגֶב south/Negev","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Three senses: cardinal-direction south (12), proper-geographic Negev region (18 dominant), and ambiguous south/Negev overlap (1). A classic direction-from-geography polysemy — the dry southern region of Israel lexicalized as both a place name and the word for 'south'."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5148","glossChirho":"נחה lead, guide","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Clean 3-way: lead/guide (28 occ), bring-to-destination (1), settle/station (2). Hifil causative gets its own sense for stationing/settling."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5676","glossChirho":"עֵבֶר beyond, side","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Spatial noun: beyond/across (18), side/direction (9), sides/edges plural (4). The 'beyond' sense dominates in construct phrases like עֵבֶר הַיַּרְדֵּן."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5712","glossChirho":"עֵדָה congregation","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Congregation-of-Israel dominant (25), company/band for non-Israelite groups (4), swarm for bees (2). The bee-swarm sense is Judg 14:8 specific."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6231","glossChirho":"עשׁק oppress","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"5-way split across voice/aspect: oppress/exploit active (15), oppressor participial (8), oppressed passive (6), overwhelm figurative (1), burdened passive-result (1)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6697","glossChirho":"צוּר rock","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Rock/cliff physical (20), Rock as divine epithet (9), flint material (1), edge-of-flint-knife (1). The divine epithet is a major theological metaphor."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6918","glossChirho":"קָדוֹשׁ holy","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"5 senses: holy divine-attribute (5), Holy-One title (14), holy-ones/saints (10), holy-things (1), holy-place (1). Holy-One as substantivized title is the dominant usage."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H8064","glossChirho":"שָׁמַיִם heavens, sky","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"6 senses differentiated by prepositional frame: heavens/sky bare (11), in-heavens locative (3), from-heaven source (2), toward-heavens directional (8), heavens-of construct (3), your/his-heavens possessive (4)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H8081","glossChirho":"שֶׁמֶן oil","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"4-way: oil substance (24), ointment/perfumed-oil (2), olive-tree metonymy (1), fatness/richness metaphorical (4). The fatness sense appears in blessings contexts."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H8252","glossChirho":"שׁקט be quiet, rest","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"Binyan-split: be-quiet/rest Qal (17), quiet adjectival (5), give-rest Hifil causative (5), quietness nominal (4)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H8641","glossChirho":"תְּרוּמָה contribution, offering","fromChirho":31,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"3 senses: contribution/sacred-offering general (25), heave-offering/priestly-portion technical (5), tribute/bribe secular (1)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G0190","glossChirho":"ἀκολουθέω follow","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Clean 2-way: follow physically/accompany (22), follow-as-disciple (8). The discipleship sense is concentrated in Jesus' call narratives."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G0615","glossChirho":"ἀποκτείνω kill","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Voice split only: kill active (23), be-killed passive (7). No metaphorical senses detected."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G2038","glossChirho":"ἐργάζομαι work, labor","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"5 senses: work/labor basic (17), do/perform-deeds moral (9), produce/bring-about (2), trade/do-business (1), wrought passive (1). The moral-deed sense is Pauline."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G3049","glossChirho":"λογίζομαι reckon, consider","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"3-way: credit/impute theological (8), consider/regard-as evaluative (14), think/reason/deliberate cognitive (8). The imputation sense is concentrated in Romans 4."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G3745","glossChirho":"ὅσος as many as","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"5 senses by grammatical function: as-many-as persons (8), as-much-as things (10), how-much exclamatory (4), as-long-as temporal (3), proportional degree (5)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G4863","glossChirho":"συνάγω gather together","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"3-way: assemble intransitive/passive (13), gather/collect transitive (15), take-in/hospitality (2). The hospitality sense (Matt 25:35,38) is a distinctive NT extension."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0034","glossChirho":"אֶבְיוֹן needy, poor","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Monosemous. All 30 senses collapse to one: needy/poor/destitute. Used both adjectivally and substantively for the social class."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0056","glossChirho":"אבל mourn","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Binyan split: mourn Qal intransitive (10), mourn Hitpael reflexive-intensive (18), cause mourning Hifil (2). The Hitpael dominates — outward mourning actions."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H0272","glossChirho":"אֲחֻזָּה possession, property","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Monosemous. All 30 senses = landed property/possession. Stable meaning across all contexts."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H2256a","glossChirho":"חֶבֶל cord, region","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"5-way polysemy from concrete→abstract: cord/rope (19), region/territory (2), portion/allotment (7), band of people (1), boundary line (1). The allotment sense derives from measuring-line practice."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3176","glossChirho":"יחל hope, wait","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"3 senses: hope/wait expectantly (28), cause to hope Piel transitive (1), writhe/pain homographic root (1). The homograph detection is notable."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H3802","glossChirho":"כָּתֵף shoulder","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"Body-part→geography: shoulder physical (11), side/slope geographic extension (16), shoulder-piece of ephod (3). The geographic sense dominates."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4217","glossChirho":"מִזְרָח east","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"2-way: east cardinal direction bare (17), rising-of-sun construct phrase (13). The construct phrase מִזְרַח הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ is a fixed expression."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H4557","glossChirho":"מִסְפָּר number","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"3 senses: number/count/tally (27), few-in-number idiomatic (2), account/narration verbal-noun from ספר (1). The fewness idiom is notable."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5352","glossChirho":"נקה be innocent, acquit","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Rich 6-way split by binyan+semantic: Nifal unpunished (7), Nifal free-from-obligation (7), Nifal cleansed (2), Nifal emptied (1), Piel acquit (10), Piel cleanse (3). Fine-grained juridical/cultic distinction."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5422","glossChirho":"נתץ tear down","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"5-way: tear-down Qal (18), demolish Piel intensive (5), be-torn-down passive (4), shatter teeth/bones (2), break person metaphorical (1)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H5982","glossChirho":"עַמּוּד pillar","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"2-way: architectural pillar/column (25), pillar of cloud/fire theophanic (5). The theophanic pillar is theologically significant."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6869b","glossChirho":"צָרָה distress, trouble","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Number split: distress singular abstract (21), troubles plural concrete instances (9)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H6951","glossChirho":"קָהָל assembly","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"2-way: assembly/congregation religious-civic (25), crowd/multitude general (5). The religious-civic sense dominates."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7125","glossChirho":"קרא meet, encounter","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Near-monosemous: meet/encounter (29), opposite/facing spatial (1). The spatial sense is a marginal extension."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7198","glossChirho":"קֶשֶׁת bow","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"2-way: bow weapon (28), rainbow covenant sign (2). The rainbow sense (Gen 9) is clearly distinguished by multilingual translations."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7225","glossChirho":"רֵאשִׁית beginning, firstfruits","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"3 senses: beginning temporal (12), firstfruits agricultural-cultic (13), chief/best/choicest (5). The firstfruits→best extension is well attested."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7676","glossChirho":"שַׁבָּת sabbath","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"5-way by grammatical/theological frame: weekly sabbath (11), sabbath-rest construct (4), sabbaths plural (5), my-sabbaths covenantal (4), its/her sabbaths possessive (6)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H7832","glossChirho":"שׂחק laugh, play","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":6,"notesChirho":"Rich 6-way: laugh-with-joy Qal (5), laugh-at/mock Qal (9), play/sport Piel (7), celebrate/merry Piel (7), jest Piel (1), mock Hifil (1). Good binyan-semantic alignment."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H8198","glossChirho":"שִׁפְחָה maidservant","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Monosemous. All 30 = maidservant/female-slave, including humble self-designation usage."}
{"lemmaChirho":"H8393","glossChirho":"תְּבוּאָה produce, crop","fromChirho":30,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"3-way: agricultural produce (24), income/revenue figurative (4), harvest-time temporal (2). Concrete→abstract extension."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G0565","glossChirho":"ἀπέρχομαι go away","fromChirho":29,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"4 senses: depart physically (25), pass-away temporal (2), spread-abroad of news (1), go-after/follow (1). Core sense dominates."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G0599","glossChirho":"ἀποθνῄσκω die","fromChirho":29,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"5-way by aspect/metaphor: die physically (14), die figuratively/spiritually (5), to-die infinitival (4), having-died participial (4), dying mortal-condition (2). The Pauline 'dying to sin' is a key metaphorical extension."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G1080","glossChirho":"γεννάω beget, be born","fromChirho":29,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"3-way by agent: beget/father active (6), be-born passive (19), give-birth mother-as-agent (4). The passive 'be born' dominates, including 'born of God/Spirit'."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G1519","glossChirho":"εἰς into, to","fromChirho":29,"toChirho":8,"notesChirho":"Highly polysemous preposition: direction (13), purpose (3), locative (2), hostile (2), reference (2), contact (3), presence (2), manner/role (2). 8 senses reflect Koine broadening."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G2097","glossChirho":"εὐαγγελίζω evangelize","fromChirho":29,"toChirho":2,"notesChirho":"Voice split: proclaim good news active/middle (23), be evangelized passive (6)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G2597","glossChirho":"καταβαίνω go down","fromChirho":29,"toChirho":3,"notesChirho":"3-way: go/come down physically (27), fall from sky (1), be brought down in judgment (1). Physical descent strongly dominant."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G2784","glossChirho":"κηρύσσω proclaim, preach","fromChirho":29,"toChirho":1,"notesChirho":"Monosemous. All 29 = proclaim/preach/herald. No semantic differentiation detected despite diverse morphological forms."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G4098","glossChirho":"πίπτω fall","fromChirho":29,"toChirho":5,"notesChirho":"5-way: fall-down/prostrate worship (7), collapse/destroyed (2), fall physically (11), stumble/fall-morally (7), fall-upon/strike (2)."}
{"lemmaChirho":"G5101","glossChirho":"τίς who, what","fromChirho":29,"toChirho":4,"notesChirho":"4-way by function: who interrogative persons (9), what/which interrogative things (12), why causal (4), someone/something indefinite (4). Clean grammatical split."}
